#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Stand Builder's backend up with one command, by calling the four that do it.

    bin/stand-up-backend --shape single|two              provision, place, register, supervise
    bin/stand-up-backend --shape single|two --activate   ...and start the jobs marked default-on
    bin/stand-up-backend --shape single|two --status     what each step reports, changing nothing
    bin/stand-up-backend --shape single|two --dry-run    say what would happen, change nothing
    bin/stand-up-backend --shape single|two --check      report only; 1 work left, 2 something refused

## THE OPERATOR NAMES THE SHAPE, AND THIS NEVER FALLS BACK FROM ONE TO THE OTHER

`--shape single` installs Builder as the operator's own account, with no
administrator. `provision-backend` runs with `--no-install`, so a missing
program stops the run with the command that installs it, and nothing reaches
for sudo. On Linux it enables lingering for the operator, so the jobs keep
running after logout; where the machine refuses, it says so and carries on.

`--shape two` installs the two-account shape, which needs the `agents` and
`reviewers` accounts and the privileged half. **It checks for them before any
step runs**, and where one is missing it stops, names what it lacked, and
leaves the machine as it found it. Quietly installing the single-account
pieces instead would hand the operator the shape they did not choose, and the
one that gives up the boundary they chose the other for.

**THIS EXISTS BECAUSE FIVE STEPS IN AN UNENFORCED ORDER IS NOT AN INSTALL.**
Each of the four commands below is complete, refuses rather than half-doing its
job, and names the next one on stdout. That is better than the prose in a shell
comment it replaced, and it is still five things an early adopter on an Azure VM
has to run in the right order, holding two facts nobody wrote down:

- **Provision precedes placement**, because placement builds a venv per deploy
  area and needs a working interpreter and `pip` to do it.
- **Registration and supervision follow placement**, because both point into a
  deploy area. `bin/register-mcp` refuses outright when the area is absent, and
  a job pointed at a missing area undoes the rule that a service serves only from
  its deploy area.

**THE TWO-ACCOUNT SHAPE RUNS THREE STEPS AS `agents` AND SUPERVISION AS ROOT.**
`place`, `link` and `register` run through `sudo -u agents -H`, so the deploy areas,
the linked commands and the MCP registration are the backend account's. `supervise`
runs root's own copy of `install-jobs`, from the commit `install-privileged` recorded,
under the cleared environment `update-privileged` uses, and renders the declarations
for `agents`. `provision` is left out: it installs packages, which needs an administrator
this shape does not run as.

`--migrate-from <operator home>` moves an existing single-account install into the
`agents` account, and `--rollback-to <operator home>` reverses that from the record the
migration wrote under the root-owned prefix. A real run is root's, and root runs only the
copy `install-privileged` extracted and recorded, invoked by its absolute path:

    sudo /opt/homebrew/bin/python3 -I /opt/builder/src/shipwright@<sha>/bin/stand-up-backend \
        --shape two --migrate-from <operator home>

Root refuses to start from any other copy, before it reads anything, unless that file, every
directory above it and the `bin`, `tools` and `jobs` trees beside it belong to root with no
group or other write bit. Both migration modes refuse before changing anything when the record
already exists, a Builder job is loaded, a checkout has uncommitted work, or a product service
names the root that is moving. Their steps are planned first, so `--dry-run` shows every one.

**It calls them; it reimplements nothing.** They already refuse rather than
half-installing, say what they did, and change nothing on a second run. This
inherits those four properties by delegation, and must not become the fifth
place any of them is invented.

## THE INTERPRETER IS THE TRAP THIS CARRIES FOR THE OPERATOR

Job declarations are TOML and `tomllib` arrived in 3.11. Ubuntu 22.04 ships
3.10 as `python3`, and installing 3.11 alongside does **not** change what
`/usr/bin/env python3` resolves to — so `bin/install-jobs` fails under the
interpreter that will run it by default, and `bin/provision-backend`'s closing
text tells the operator to type `python3.11 bin/install-jobs` by hand. That
instruction is correct and it is exactly the kind of thing an installer should
not leave to a person.

**This picks an interpreter and PRINTS the one it picked**, for every step that
does not simply inherit this process. `install-backend` is also given it as
`--python`, because that flag defaults to whatever interpreter happened to
invoke it and a deploy venv built with 22.04's stock 3.10 is a worse starting
point than one built with the 3.11 `provision-backend` just installed.

## EVERY MODE'S EXIT CODE MEANS THE SAME THING

`0` nothing to do or everything done, `1` work is left, `2` something
refused. **That holds for `--check` and for a real run.**

**Under `--dry-run` it cannot hold at all**: no tool reports pending work
through its exit code there, so a `0` says only that nothing crashed.

**Under `--status` it holds for half of them, which is worse than neither.**
`provision-backend` exits `1` for a missing package and `install-backend` for a
deploy area that is missing or unreadable — staleness is a `--check` finding,
not a `--status` one; `register-mcp` and `install-jobs` return `0` whatever
they printed, including over `NOT INSTALLED`. So a `0` from those two
is not a clean bill of health, and this reports what each one SAID rather than
leaning on the code. **A `1` means different things in different modes from the
SAME tool**, which is why `verdict_of` takes the mode and each step carries a
`work_left_modes` tuple read off that tool's own returns.

That divergence is also why the chain stops on **any** non-zero rather than on
`2` alone: a step that dies of an uncaught exception exits `1` and one killed by
a signal is negative, and continuing past either installs onto a machine that
cannot run what comes next.

**A skipped supervision step counts as work left**, because it is. A run that
prints "SUPERVISION IS NOT INSTALLED" and exits `0` is a report contradicting
itself, which is the defect this whole command exists one level up to refuse.

## WHAT A GREEN RUN DOES NOT MEAN

**A green supervise step means the declarations in `jobs/` were rendered and
installed for this machine's supervisor**, and with `--activate` that the
default-on jobs were started and checked once. A fork that ships no
declarations has no `jobs/` directory, or none with a `.toml` in it; when no
`--jobs` is given, this checks the default location first and reports that
the step was skipped, rather than surfacing `no such directory`, which reads
as a misconfiguration the operator caused. **When `--jobs` IS given, the step
runs and `install-jobs` refuses in its own words**, because a path the
operator named and got wrong is exactly the thing they should be told about.

**Installed is not started.** `install-jobs` writes the units; it starts only
the default-on ones, and only with `--activate`, which this passes through.
Without it the jobs are on the machine and not running.

## What it never does

**It writes no credentials of its own.** The Library's job declaration mints
Orient's two and the work-queue tools' one, once each, and no installer
copies or overwrites a credential. The one exception is `bootstrap-library`,
which runs after `supervise` and makes the operator the Library's first
person: it is `bootstrap_library.py`, the Library's own one-time command,
that mints the operator's administrator credential, once, and this never
prints or logs the raw token. It provisions no chat webhooks, Slack's
included, although `agent-platform/install.sh` lists them as a manual step:
an installer that sets up a surface nobody reads is worse than one that omits
it.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import contextlib
import grp
import json
import os
import pwd
import re
import shutil
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
from pathlib import Path

PROG = "stand-up-backend"
REPO = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent


# `work_left_modes` NAMES THE MODES IN WHICH THAT TOOL'S `1` MEANS PENDING WORK
# RATHER THAN A CRASH, and it is per-tool because the four disagree. Read off
# each tool's own returns rather than assumed from the flag name, because
# `--status` is where they diverge most:
#
#   provision-backend  `return 1 if (missing and args.check)`      (line 317)
#   write-operator-config `--check` and `--status` both `1 if`
#                      absent (268, 272)
#   install-backend    `--check` 1 when stale (988); `--status`
#                      returns `1 if missing` from status()        (866)
#   register-mcp       `--check` 1 when unready (646); `--status`
#                      returns 0 whatever it printed               (616)
#   install-jobs       `--check` 1 when it would write (main);
#                      `--status` only ever returns 0 (report_status); a REAL
#                      run is `activation(...) or (1 if skipped)`,
#                      so it returns 1 when a live unit differs
#                      from its declaration and was skipped — the
#                      common case — when an activation command
#                      failed, or when a job it activated is still
#                      reported down afterwards                    (activation)
#   bootstrap-library  `--check` and `--status` both `1 if` person_id is
#                      absent (main); a real run has no non-crash `1` at
#                      all — it either writes person_id or refuses
#
# Under `--dry-run` no tool reports pending work through its code at all, so a
# `1` there is an uncaught exception in every case and the mode appears nowhere
# below. `None` means a real run, and only `install-jobs` has a non-crash `1`
# there.
STEPS = (
    {
        "name": "provision",
        "tool": "provision-backend",
        "why": "install what the platform lacks, verified by running each program",
        "modes": {"dry-run": "--dry-run", "check": "--check", "status": "--check"},
        "work_left_modes": ("check", "status"),
        "needs_311": False,
    },
    {
        "name": "operator-config",
        "tool": "write-operator-config",
        "why": "write the operator identity file every backend service reads before it starts",
        # **BEFORE `place` AND NOT AFTER.** `install-backend --seed-registry`
        # seeds the Library's roster inside the deploy area it builds, and the
        # roster loader `jobs/library.toml` runs at the Library's first start
        # already reads `operator_marker` from this file.
        "modes": {"dry-run": "--dry-run", "check": "--check", "status": "--status"},
        "work_left_modes": ("check", "status"),
        "needs_311": False,
    },
    {
        "name": "place",
        "tool": "install-backend",
        "why": "put the code where a service can serve it, from a published ref",
        "modes": {"dry-run": "--dry-run", "check": "--check", "status": "--status"},
        "work_left_modes": ("check", "status"),
        "needs_311": False,
    },
    {
        "name": "link",
        "tool": "link-commands",
        "why": "put the core commands where an operator can type their names",
        # **AFTER `place` AND NOT BEFORE IT**, because this links into the area
        # that step creates and refuses rather than writing a link to a file
        # that is not there yet.
        "modes": {"dry-run": "--dry-run", "check": "--check", "status": "--status"},
        # Read off this tool's own returns, as the note above requires rather
        # than assumed from the flag names: `--check` returns 1 when a command
        # is unlinked OR when every link exists and the directory is not on
        # PATH (both are work left), and `status()` returns `1 if missing`,
        # where "missing" counts an occupied destination as well as an absent
        # one. Neither returns a non-crash 1 in a real run.
        "work_left_modes": ("check", "status"),
        "needs_311": False,
    },
    {
        "name": "register",
        "tool": "register-mcp",
        "why": "register the kb and ops MCP servers, so a session has a Library",
        "modes": {"dry-run": "--dry-run", "check": "--check", "status": "--status"},
        "work_left_modes": ("check",),
        "needs_311": False,
    },
    {
        "name": "supervise",
        "tool": "install-jobs",
        "why": "render and install the backend's jobs for this machine's supervisor",
        # `--check` here is REAL, added to install-jobs in this change. Mapped
        # to `--dry-run` it could not answer the question: that mode exits 0
        # whether it would write ten units or none, so `--check` reported `ok`
        # for supervise on a machine where nothing was installed.
        "modes": {"dry-run": "--dry-run", "check": "--check", "status": "--status"},
        "work_left_modes": ("check", None),
        "needs_311": True,
    },
    {
        "name": "library-person",
        "tool": "bootstrap-library",
        "why": "make the operator the Library's first person, once",
        # **AFTER `supervise` AND NOT BEFORE IT.** `jobs/library.toml`'s own
        # `prepare` commands run `alembic upgrade head`, which is what creates
        # `bootstrap_record` — the table this reads and writes. Nothing before
        # `supervise` has run that migration.
        "modes": {"dry-run": "--dry-run", "check": "--check", "status": "--status"},
        "work_left_modes": ("check", "status"),
        "needs_311": True,
    },
)


class Refused(Exception):
    """Something the operator has to decide about. Never a stack trace."""


SHAPES = {
    "single": "one account, the operator's own, with no administrator",
    "two": "the agents and reviewers accounts and a root-owned privileged half",
}

# What the two-account shape needs before anything is installed for it.
TWO_ACCOUNT_USERS = ("agents", "reviewers")
PRIVILEGED_INSTALLER = Path("bin", "install-privileged")

# THE BACKEND'S ACCOUNT, AND THE ROOT-OWNED TREE THE PRIVILEGED HALF INSTALLS.
# `sudo -u agents -H` is how the steps run as it: `-H` makes its home the HOME
# the steps see. The root half is read from the record install-privileged
# writes, `name sha` per line, and runs from its own extraction under
# PRIVILEGED_PREFIX, never from the agents checkout (step 3's rule).
AGENTS = "agents"
SUDO = "/usr/bin/sudo"
AS_AGENTS = [SUDO, "-u", AGENTS, "-H"]
# BY ABSOLUTE PATH wherever root runs them, as SSH_KEYGEN is: a bare name resolves through PATH,
# and an earlier PATH directory the operator or agents can write would supply the program root runs.
GIT = "/usr/bin/git"
LAUNCHCTL = "/bin/launchctl"
PRIVILEGED_PREFIX = Path("/opt/builder")
INSTALLED_RECORD = PRIVILEGED_PREFIX / "src" / "installed"
# The cleared environment update-privileged runs root's tools under, so no
# variable the operator exported reaches a process that runs as root.
CLEAN_ENV = ["/usr/bin/env", "-i", "PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin", "HOME=/var/root"]
# The same interpreters update-privileged names. The Homebrew one is the
# operator's, which step 2 accepts in the design: root runs it, and `agents`
# is in neither `admin` nor `staff`, so `agents` cannot change it.
ROOT_INTERPRETERS = {"darwin": "/opt/homebrew/bin/python3", "linux": "/usr/bin/python3"}
SHA = re.compile(r"[0-9a-f]{40}")


def _account_exists(name: str) -> bool:
    try:
        pwd.getpwnam(name)
    except KeyError:
        return False
    return True


def installed_sha(record_text: str | None, name: str) -> str | None:
    """The commit install-privileged recorded for `name`, or None.

    The record is what root reads to find its own tree, so only a full 40-hex
    SHA on a line naming exactly `name` counts. Anything else is no answer.
    """
    if record_text is None:
        return None
    for line in record_text.splitlines():
        fields = line.split()
        if len(fields) == 2 and fields[0] == name and SHA.fullmatch(fields[1]):
            return fields[1]
    return None


def _read_record(path: Path) -> str | None:
    try:
        return path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError:
        return None


# --- the copy root runs --------------------------------------------------------

RUNNING_COPY = re.compile(r"^(?P<prefix>.*)/src/shipwright@[0-9a-f]{40}/bin/stand-up-backend$")
# The trees whose files root imports or executes, beside the script.
ROOT_OWNED_TREES = ("bin", "tools", "jobs")
# Root's files belong to uid 0, and every component above a root-run file is checked up to `/`.
ROOT_UID = 0
ROOT_TOP = Path("/")


def _owned_by(path: Path, owner: int) -> str | None:
    try:
        st = os.lstat(path)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return f"{path} is missing"
    if stat.S_ISLNK(st.st_mode) or st.st_uid != owner or st.st_mode & 0o022:
        return f"{path} is not owned by uid {owner} alone, or another account can write it"
    return None


def tree_problem(script: Path, owner: int, top: Path) -> str | None:
    """Why root must not run `script` and the trees beside it, or None when it may.

    **ROOT RUNS ONLY FILES THAT AN ACCOUNT RUNNING AGENT SESSIONS CANNOT CHANGE.** Every component
    from `script` up to `top` must belong to `owner` with no group or other write bit and must not
    be a symlink. So must everything under the `bin`, `tools` and `jobs` trees beside `script`.
    """
    for part in [script, *script.parents]:
        if part == top or top in part.parents:
            problem = _owned_by(part, owner)
            if problem:
                return problem
    tree = script.parent.parent
    for name in ROOT_OWNED_TREES:
        base = tree / name
        problem = _owned_by(base, owner)
        if problem:
            return problem
        # A directory that cannot be listed is not skipped: os.walk would drop it and its entries unchecked.
        unlistable: list[OSError] = []
        for dirpath, dirs, files in os.walk(base, onerror=unlistable.append):
            for entry in [*dirs, *files]:
                problem = _owned_by(Path(dirpath, entry), owner)
                if problem:
                    return problem
        if unlistable:
            return (f"{unlistable[0].filename} cannot be listed, so its entries cannot be checked "
                    f"({unlistable[0].strerror})")
    return None


def root_copy_problem(script: str, owner: int = 0, *, prefix: Path = PRIVILEGED_PREFIX,
                      top: Path = Path("/")) -> str | None:
    """Why root must not run `script`, or None when it may.

    **ROOT RUNS ONLY THE COPY install-privileged EXTRACTED.** `REPO` is computed from the same file,
    so every import root makes comes from the tree `tree_problem` checks.
    """
    match = RUNNING_COPY.match(script)
    if match is None or match.group("prefix") != str(prefix):
        return f"the file is not at {prefix}/src/shipwright@<sha>/bin/stand-up-backend"
    return tree_problem(Path(script), owner, top)


def two_shape_lacks(repo: Path = REPO, exists=_account_exists,
                    record: Path = INSTALLED_RECORD) -> list[str]:
    """What the two-account shape needs and this machine lacks, one line each.

    **THE ACCOUNTS ARE ASKED OF THE DIRECTORY, NOT OF `provision-accounts
    --check`**, because that command needs root for every mode and this runs
    as the operator. Whether an account that exists has the right shape is
    `provision-accounts`' question, which the refusal tells the operator to
    ask. The privileged half's installer is looked for in this release, which
    is where a release that cannot install it says so. The privileged half
    itself is looked for in the record install-privileged writes, since the
    steps this runs cannot work without the launcher it installs.
    """
    lacks = [
        f"the `{name}` account, which `sudo bin/provision-accounts` creates"
        for name in TWO_ACCOUNT_USERS if not exists(name)
    ]
    if not (repo / PRIVILEGED_INSTALLER).is_file():
        lacks.append(
            f"the privileged half: this release has no `{PRIVILEGED_INSTALLER}` "
            f"to install it with")
    if installed_sha(_read_record(record), "shipwright") is None:
        lacks.append(
            f"the privileged half is not installed: {record} names no shipwright "
            f"commit. Run `sudo bin/install-privileged` as the steps in the "
            f"installer plan say")
    return lacks


def refuse_two_shape(lacks: list[str]) -> Refused:
    listed = "\n".join(f"  - {line}" for line in lacks)
    return Refused(
        f"the two-account shape cannot be installed on this machine. It lacks:\n"
        f"{listed}\n"
        f"Nothing was installed or changed, and the single-account shape was not "
        f"installed in its place. To install as one account instead, run with "
        f"`--shape single`.")


LINGER_REFUSED = (
    "lingering could not be enabled for {user}, so Builder's jobs will stop when "
    "{user} logs out and start again at the next login. Whoever administers this "
    "machine can enable it with `loginctl enable-linger {user}`.")


def lingers_before(step: dict, mode: str | None, shape: str,
                   platform: str = sys.platform) -> bool:
    """Whether to enable lingering before running `step`: just before the jobs
    are installed, in a real single-account run, on Linux. Every reporting mode
    changes nothing, and the two-account shape's jobs run as `agents`."""
    return (step["name"] == "supervise" and mode is None and shape == "single"
            and platform.startswith("linux"))


def enable_linger(user: str, run=subprocess.run) -> str | None:
    """Enable lingering for `user` on Linux, or the sentence saying it was refused.

    **A REFUSAL IS REPORTED, NOT RAISED.** Without lingering the user's jobs
    stop at logout, which is a weaker install rather than a broken one, and a
    machine that refuses it is one the operator may not administer, which is
    the case this shape exists for."""
    try:
        done = run(["loginctl", "enable-linger", user],
                   capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60, check=False)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return LINGER_REFUSED.format(user=user)
    if done.returncode != 0:
        return LINGER_REFUSED.format(user=user)
    return None


def _has_tomllib(interpreter: str) -> bool:
    # `-I` FOR EVERY PROBE: `-c` puts the current directory first on sys.path, so a `tomllib.py`
    # planted there would run as root. Isolated mode drops the cwd and PYTHON* from sys.path.
    try:
        return subprocess.run(
            [interpreter, "-I", "-c", "import tomllib"],
            capture_output=True, timeout=60, check=False,
        ).returncode == 0
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return False


def best_interpreter() -> str | None:
    """An interpreter with `tomllib`, or None — never a refusal from in here, except under root.

    Returning None rather than raising is what lets a REPORTING mode say which
    step cannot run and still print the other three. Raising from inside the
    step loop threw the whole report away on the one machine the docstring is
    about: a fresh 22.04 box, first `--dry-run`, before `python3.11` exists.

    **ROOT NAMES NO INTERPRETER IT WAS NOT GIVEN.** Its own interpreter is the one the
    sudo command named by absolute path, and PATH is never searched: an earlier PATH
    directory an agent can write would supply the program root runs. Root refuses,
    naming what it needed, rather than returning None.
    """
    if os.geteuid() == 0:
        if os.path.isabs(sys.executable) and _has_tomllib(sys.executable):
            return sys.executable
        raise Refused(
            f"root has no Python 3.11 or later at the absolute path it was started from "
            f"({sys.executable}), and it never searches PATH for one. Run the installed copy under "
            f"such an interpreter, named by its absolute path, as the usage in this file shows. "
            f"Nothing was changed.")
    if _has_tomllib(sys.executable):
        return sys.executable
    for name in ("python3.11", "python3.12", "python3.13", "python3.14"):
        found = shutil.which(name)
        if found and _has_tomllib(found):
            return found
    return None


def interpreter_for(step: dict, best: str | None) -> str:
    if not step["needs_311"]:
        return sys.executable
    if best is None:
        raise Refused(
            "no interpreter with `tomllib` was found, and job declarations are "
            "TOML. `tomllib` arrived in Python 3.11; Ubuntu 22.04 ships 3.10 as "
            "`python3`, and installing 3.11 alongside does not change what "
            "`/usr/bin/env python3` resolves to."
        )
    return best


def declarations_dir(args) -> Path:
    return args.jobs if args.jobs is not None else REPO / "jobs"


def supervision_skipped(args) -> bool:
    """Whether to skip `install-jobs` rather than let it refuse.

    ONLY FOR THE DEFAULT DIRECTORY. `install-jobs` refuses with `no such
    directory`, which is the wrong thing to show for a fork that ships no
    declarations — that is the fork's choice, not the operator's mistake.
    **But a `--jobs` the operator typed and got wrong IS their mistake**, and
    skipping it would tell them the repository ships no declarations, which is
    false about the path they named. So an explicit `--jobs` always runs the
    step and lets the tool speak for itself.
    """
    if args.jobs is not None:
        return False
    directory = declarations_dir(args)
    return not (directory.is_dir() and any(directory.glob("*.toml")))


def resolve_operator_name(explicit: str | None, best: str | None) -> tuple[str, str]:
    """`(name, source)`, resolved by running `bin/bootstrap-library
    --print-operator-name` as a subprocess, in THIS process's own context —
    the operator's, never through `AS_AGENTS`.

    **ONLY FOR `--shape two`.** Its `library-person` step runs as `agents`
    (`runs_as`), and `bootstrap-library`'s own fallback chain, run from
    there, would read `agents`' git config and account name rather than the
    operator's. Resolving it here, before the step drops to `agents`, and
    always passing the result as `--operator-name`, is what keeps the
    Library's first administrator named after the person installing it.
    `bin/bootstrap-library` is the one place the chain itself is defined;
    this never duplicates it.

    **RUN UNDER THE SAME 3.11 INTERPRETER THE `needs_311` STEPS USE**,
    refusing with the same message when none is found: `bootstrap-library`
    cannot even be imported under an older one, since it reads
    `jobs/library.toml`, which needs `tomllib`. `--print-operator-name`
    resolves the name and exits before anything that needs `--root` or the
    jobs directory — no database, no operator config file.
    """
    interpreter = interpreter_for({"needs_311": True}, best)
    argv = [interpreter, str(REPO / "bin" / "bootstrap-library"), "--print-operator-name"]
    if explicit is not None:
        argv += ["--operator-name", explicit]
    try:
        done = subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30, check=False)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as exc:
        raise Refused(
            f"could not resolve the operator name: {exc}. Nothing was installed.") from exc
    if done.returncode != 0:
        raise Refused(
            f"bootstrap-library --print-operator-name failed: "
            f"{(done.stderr or '').strip()}. Nothing was installed.")
    lines = done.stdout.splitlines()
    if len(lines) != 2:
        raise Refused(
            f"bootstrap-library --print-operator-name printed "
            f"{len(lines)} line(s), expected 2: {done.stdout!r}. Nothing was installed.")
    return lines[0], lines[1]


def argv_for(step: dict, args, mode: str | None, best: str | None) -> list[str]:
    """The exact command line for one step, from what that tool accepts.

    Each tool takes a different subset, so this is a table rather than a loop:
    passing `--deploy` to `provision-backend`, which has no such flag, would
    make argparse exit 2 and be reported as that step refusing.
    `tools/test_stand_up_backend.py` reads each real tool's own
    `add_argument` calls and asserts every flag named here is one it accepts.
    """
    argv = [interpreter_for(step, best), str(REPO / "bin" / step["tool"])]
    if mode is not None:
        argv.append(step["modes"][mode])
    elif step["name"] == "provision" and args.shape == "single":
        # The single-account shape has no administrator: name what is missing
        # and how to install it, never sudo.
        argv.append("--no-install")
    if step["name"] in ("place", "register", "library-person") and args.deploy is not None:
        argv += ["--deploy", str(args.deploy)]
    if step["name"] == "place" and best is not None:
        # `--python` defaults to whatever invoked the tool. On 22.04 that is
        # the stock 3.10, and a deploy venv built with it is a worse floor than
        # one built with the interpreter provision just installed.
        argv += ["--python", best]
    if step["name"] == "place" and args.seed_registry:
        argv += ["--seed-registry"]
    if step["name"] == "supervise":
        argv += ["--jobs", str(declarations_dir(args))]
        if args.shape == "single":
            # The declarations name `agents` for the two-account shape; the operator runs them here.
            argv += ["--single-account"]
        if args.deploy is not None:
            argv += ["--deploy", str(args.deploy)]
        if args.activate:
            argv += ["--activate"]
    if step["name"] == "library-person":
        argv += ["--jobs", str(declarations_dir(args))]
        if args.shape == "two":
            # `--shape single` runs this step as the operator, and
            # `bootstrap-library` resolves the name for itself there,
            # exactly as on main. `--shape two` runs it as `agents`, so the
            # name is resolved here instead, as a subprocess in this
            # process's own context, and always passed through.
            name, source = resolve_operator_name(args.operator_name, best)
            print(f"{PROG}: library-person's operator name: {name!r} (from {source})")
            argv += ["--operator-name", name]
        elif args.operator_name is not None:
            argv += ["--operator-name", args.operator_name]
    return argv


def steps_for(shape: str) -> tuple:
    """The steps this shape runs, in order.

    **THE TWO-ACCOUNT SHAPE LEAVES OUT `provision`.** That step installs
    packages, which needs an administrator, and this shape runs as `agents`
    and as root, never as the operator. Whether the interpreter exists is what
    `place` refuses on, and `place` says which command installs it.
    """
    if shape == "two":
        return tuple(step for step in STEPS if step["name"] != "provision")
    return STEPS


def runs_as(step: dict, shape: str) -> str:
    """Who runs one step: the operator, `agents`, or root's own copy.

    In the single-account shape everything is the operator's. In the two-account
    shape the backend's steps are `agents`', and supervision is root's, because a
    system-scope job is installed by root whatever account it runs as.
    """
    if shape == "single":
        return "operator"
    return "root" if step["name"] == "supervise" else "agents"


def agents_home() -> str:
    """The home the directory service reports for `agents`, never a literal."""
    try:
        return pwd.getpwnam(AGENTS).pw_dir
    except KeyError:
        raise Refused(f"the `{AGENTS}` account does not exist. Run "
                      f"`sudo bin/provision-accounts`. Nothing was installed.")


def agents_builder_root(home: str, run=None) -> str:
    """The Builder root `agents` runs with, read by `agents` from its own linked command.

    **ROOT DOES NOT ASK `builder-root`.** That command is asked only by the
    account that runs it, for the reason install-jobs gives: a run as root never
    executes a program another account could have placed. So `agents` answers,
    and root is handed the answer as `--root`.
    """
    run = run or _run_quiet
    link = f"{home}/.local/bin/builder-root"
    code, out = run([*AS_AGENTS, link])
    root = out.strip()
    if code != 0 or not os.path.isabs(root) or "\n" in root:
        raise Refused(
            f"could not read the Builder root from `{link}` as `{AGENTS}`. The "
            f"link step places that command, so it has to run first. Nothing was "
            f"installed for supervision.")
    return root


def root_supervise(args, mode: str | None, run=None) -> tuple[list[str], list[str]]:
    """The root-run supervision command, and the arguments a person reads for it.

    **ROOT RUNS ITS OWN COPY, FROM THE COMMIT STEP 3 INSTALLED, UNDER THE CLEARED
    ENVIRONMENT update-privileged uses.** The tree is found through the record
    install-privileged writes, so this never reads the agents checkout that
    invoked it. `--home` and `--root` name `agents`' home and root, because the
    job declarations are rendered for that account, and `--jobs` names the
    declarations in the same tree the code runs from.

    **THAT TREE IS CHECKED AS THE RUNNING COPY IS, BEFORE ROOT RUNS ANYTHING IN IT.** The record names
    a commit that can differ from the copy the operator typed, so the running copy's check does not
    cover it.
    """
    sha = installed_sha(_read_record(INSTALLED_RECORD), "shipwright")
    if sha is None:
        raise Refused(f"{INSTALLED_RECORD} names no shipwright commit, so root has no "
                      f"copy to supervise from. Nothing was installed.")
    tree = PRIVILEGED_PREFIX / "src" / f"shipwright@{sha}"
    tool = tree / "bin" / "install-jobs"
    if not tool.is_file():
        raise Refused(f"{tool} is missing, so root has no copy of install-jobs to run. "
                      f"Re-run install-privileged. Nothing was installed.")
    problem = tree_problem(tool, ROOT_UID, ROOT_TOP)
    if problem:
        raise Refused(f"{tool} is not a copy root may run: {problem}. Re-run install-privileged. "
                      f"Nothing was installed.")
    home = agents_home()
    # THE LOGS OF THE ACCOUNT'S JOBS GO UNDER THE ROOT-OWNED PREFIX, not the account's home, so root never
    # opens a log path the account can re-point (see install-jobs' `resolve`).
    flags = ["--home", home, "--root", agents_builder_root(home, run),
             "--jobs", str(tree / "jobs"), "--log-dir", str(PRIVILEGED_PREFIX / "var" / "log")]
    if args.deploy is not None:
        flags += ["--deploy", str(args.deploy)]
    if mode is not None:
        flags.append({"dry-run": "--dry-run", "check": "--check", "status": "--status"}[mode])
    if args.activate:
        flags.append("--activate")
    interpreter = ROOT_INTERPRETERS["darwin" if sys.platform == "darwin" else "linux"]
    shown = [str(tool), *flags]
    return [SUDO, *CLEAN_ENV, interpreter, "-I", "-u", *shown], shown


def launch(step: dict, args, mode: str | None, best: str | None, run=None) -> tuple[list[str], list[str]]:
    """The full command for one step, and the part of it a person reads.

    **THE PREFIX IS THE ONLY DIFFERENCE BETWEEN THE SHAPES' STEPS.** `argv_for`
    builds the tool's own command line, which tests read, and this adds the
    account it runs as in front of it. Supervision in the two-account shape is
    not a prefix on the agents' command, since it runs root's copy instead.
    """
    who = runs_as(step, args.shape)
    if who == "root":
        return root_supervise(args, mode, run)
    argv = argv_for(step, args, mode, best)
    prefix = AS_AGENTS if who == "agents" else []
    return [*prefix, argv[0], "-u", *argv[1:]], argv[1:]


def run_step(step: dict, args, mode: str | None, best: str | None) -> dict:
    """Run one step, ECHOING its output as it arrives, and keep the exit code.

    **Echoed rather than captured, because the waits are long and silent.**
    `install-backend` prints "building the venv… can take a while — do not
    start a second run" before it does exactly that; captured, that warning
    reaches the operator only after the wait it was meant to cover, while apt
    and pip run silent for minutes.

    The exit code is kept rather than translated: the four tools share one
    convention (0 did it, 1 work left, 2 refused) and a caller here needs to
    tell a refusal from a report.
    """
    try:
        full, shown = launch(step, args, mode, best)
    except Refused as exc:
        # NOT raised out of the loop. A reporting mode must still show the
        # other steps; a real run stops on it like any other refusal.
        return {"step": step, "argv": None, "code": 2, "stdout": "",
                "stderr": str(exc), "unrunnable": True}

    print(f"→ {step['name']}: {' '.join(shown)}")
    lines: list[str] = []
    # STDERR GOES TO A FILE, NOT A SECOND PIPE. Draining stdout to exhaustion
    # and only then reading a stderr PIPE deadlocks: a step that fills the
    # stderr buffer blocks writing, so it never closes stdout, so the loop
    # below never ends and the run hangs for ever. Measured, not reasoned —
    # a step writing 400 KB to stderr hung this command until it was killed.
    # A file has no such buffer, and needs no second thread to drain.
    with tempfile.TemporaryFile("w+", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as errors:
        # `-u` OR THE ECHO IS NOT LIVE. Python block-buffers stdout when it is
        # a pipe, so install-backend's "do not start a second run" warning
        # arrives with everything else once the step ENDS — after the wait it
        # exists to cover. Measured: print, sleep 3, print delivers both lines
        # at 3.0s without it and the first at 0.0s with it.
        child = subprocess.Popen(
            full, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=errors, text=True,
        )
        assert child.stdout is not None
        for line in child.stdout:
            sys.stdout.write(line)
            # FLUSHED, OR THE ECHO IS STILL NOT LIVE. `-u` unbuffers the CHILD;
            # this process block-buffers its own stdout whenever that is a pipe,
            # which is how an operator sees it under `tee`, in CI, or through
            # any wrapper. Measured: without this the early warning arrived at
            # 3.2s of a 3s wait — after the thing it exists to cover.
            sys.stdout.flush()
            lines.append(line)
        child.wait()
        errors.seek(0)
        stderr = errors.read()
    print()
    return {
        "step": step,
        "argv": shown,
        "code": child.returncode,
        "stdout": "".join(lines),
        "stderr": stderr,
        "unrunnable": False,
    }


def verdict_of(result: dict, mode: str | None) -> str:
    """What one step's exit code means, WHICH DEPENDS ON THE MODE IT RAN IN.

    A `1` is an uncaught exception in a real run and a report of pending work
    under `--check`, and the same tool produces both. Labelled by the step's
    name alone, every reporting-mode `1` read as `exit 1` — so on the fresh
    machine this command exists for, `--check` printed `exit 1  provision` and
    `exit 1  place` where the honest word is `work left`, with no stderr to
    explain it, because those tools report missing packages and a stale deploy
    area exactly that way. `STEPS[*]["work_left_modes"]` carries the per-tool
    answer; it stays a crash in a real run.
    """
    code = result["code"]
    if code == 0:
        return "ok"
    if code == 2:
        return "REFUSED"
    if code < 0:
        return f"killed ({-code})"
    if code == 1:
        left = mode in result["step"]["work_left_modes"]
        return "work left" if left else "exit 1"
    return f"exit {code}"


def blocks_from(results: list[dict]) -> list[tuple[str, str]]:
    """Each step's own closing words, attributed, VERBATIM and not merged.

    **These are four tools' text, not one list**, and the shape differs: only
    `register-mcp` prints a clean outstanding list after its report.
    `provision-backend` puts a summary line there first, `install-backend
    --check` returns before printing its list at all, and `install-jobs` has no
    such list, so its block is whatever followed its first blank line.

    **They also overlap and can contradict the run.** `provision-backend` says
    supervision is installed separately by `bin/install-jobs`, and
    `install-backend` says the MCP servers are not registered by it — both true
    of those tools alone and both describing steps this command has just run.
    De-duplicating would mean parsing four formats that are free to change, so
    the report says what these blocks are instead of pretending they are one
    curated list.
    """
    out = []
    for result in results:
        text = result["stdout"]
        if "\n\n" not in text:
            continue
        tail = text.split("\n\n", 1)[1].strip()
        if tail:
            out.append((result["step"]["name"], tail))
    return out


def report(results: list[dict], args, stopped: dict | None, best: str | None,
           mode: str | None) -> None:
    total = len(steps_for(args.shape))
    print(f"{PROG}: {'macos' if sys.platform == 'darwin' else 'linux'}")
    if best is not None and best != sys.executable:
        did = "would use" if mode is not None else "used"
        print(f"  (supervise {did} {best}, and place was given it as --python)")
    for result in results:
        step = result["step"]
        print(f"  {verdict_of(result, mode):<12} {step['name']:<10} {step['why']}")
    if supervision_skipped(args):
        print(f"  {'not built':<12} {'supervise':<10} "
              f"this repository ships no job declarations — see below")
        print(f"  {'not built':<12} {'library-person':<10} "
              f"no job declarations means no Library job to find a database in")
    print()

    # EVERY refusal's reason, not only the one that stopped the chain. In a
    # reporting mode nothing stops, so `stopped` is None and these would
    # otherwise print `REFUSED place` and never say why.
    for result in results:
        if result["code"] != 0 and (result["stderr"] or "").strip():
            print(f"what `{result['step']['name']}` said:")
            for line in result["stderr"].strip().splitlines():
                print(f"    {line}")
            print()

    if stopped is not None:
        print(f"stopped at the `{stopped['step']['name']}` step "
              f"({verdict_of(stopped, mode)}). Nothing after it ran.")
    elif all(r["code"] == 0 for r in results) and not supervision_skipped(args):
        # ONLY --check EARNS THE WORD SUCCESS, because only there does every
        # step's 0 mean nothing is pending. Under --dry-run no tool reports
        # pending work by its code — register-mcp --dry-run exits 0 while
        # printing that a real run would refuse. Under --status two of the four
        # DO report it (provision and place exit 1), but register-mcp and
        # install-jobs return 0 whatever they printed, install-jobs over NOT
        # INSTALLED, so an all-zero --status still does not mean settled.
        # Saying "reported success" in either mode contradicts the lines above.
        if mode in ("dry-run", "status"):
            print(f"every step ran ({len(results)} of {total}); these modes "
                  f"exit 0 whatever is pending — read the report, not the code")
        else:
            print(f"every step reported success ({len(results)} of {total} ran)")
    else:
        left = [r["step"]["name"] for r in results if r["code"] != 0]
        if supervision_skipped(args):
            left += ["supervise", "library-person"]
        print(f"work left in: {', '.join(left)}")


def outstanding(args, blocks, mode: str | None, supervise_ok: bool) -> list[str]:
    lines: list[str] = []
    if supervision_skipped(args):
        lines += [
            (
                "SUPERVISION IS NOT INSTALLED, AND A BACKEND WITHOUT IT DOES NOT "
                "START. `bin/install-jobs` is complete, and this repository "
                "ships no job declarations, so there is nothing for it to read. "
                "This is not something you have misconfigured:"
            ),
            f"    expected declarations in: {declarations_dir(args)}",
            "",
        ]
    elif not args.activate and mode is None and supervise_ok:
        lines += [
            (
                "The jobs are INSTALLED AND NOT STARTED. `install-jobs` starts the "
                "default-on ones only with `--activate`, which this did not pass:"
            ),
            f"    {PROG} --activate",
            "",
        ]
    if mode == "dry-run":
        lines += [
            (
                "EVERY PREDICTION AFTER THE FIRST STEP IS MADE AGAINST THIS "
                "MACHINE AS IT IS NOW, not against what the earlier steps would "
                "have done. On a fresh machine the `register` step will predict a "
                "refusal, because nothing has been placed for it to point at; a "
                "real run places first and it does not refuse."
            ),
            "",
        ]
    if blocks:
        lines += [
            (
                "Each block below is that tool's OWN closing text, verbatim. They "
                "are not one curated list: they overlap, and one may tell you to "
                "do something this command has already done."
            ),
            "",
        ]
    for name, block in blocks:
        lines.append(f"from the `{name}` step:")
        lines += [f"    {line}" if line.strip() else "" for line in block.splitlines()]
        lines.append("")
    if not lines:
        lines.append("nothing outstanding was reported by any step.")
    return lines


HOST_KEY = Path("/etc/ssh/ssh_host_ed25519_key.pub")
SSH_ACCESS_GROUP = "com.apple.access_ssh"
# BY ABSOLUTE PATH, NEVER FROM PATH: the fingerprint this prints is what a
# person trusts a host on, and an earlier, writable PATH directory such as
# ~/.local/bin could otherwise supply an `ssh-keygen` that prints an
# attacker's. Both macOS and Debian's openssh-client install it here.
SSH_KEYGEN = "/usr/bin/ssh-keygen"
DSMEMBERUTIL = "/usr/bin/dsmemberutil"


def _run_quiet(argv: list[str]) -> tuple[int, str]:
    try:
        done = subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30, check=False)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return 1, ""
    return done.returncode, done.stdout


def ssh_allows(user: str, run=_run_quiet) -> bool | None:
    """Whether macOS Remote Login lets `user` in: True, False, or None when
    this cannot tell. Remote Login limited to some users keeps them in
    `com.apple.access_ssh`, and an account outside it is refused before any
    password is asked."""
    if sys.platform != "darwin":
        return None
    # `dsmemberutil`, NOT `dscl ... GroupMembership`, because "Only these
    # users: Administrators" is stored as a nested group that GroupMembership
    # never lists: read that way, an administrator who logs in over ssh every
    # day was reported as refused.
    code, out = run([DSMEMBERUTIL, "checkmembership", "-U", user,
                     "-G", SSH_ACCESS_GROUP])
    if code != 0:
        # No such group: Remote Login is open to every account, or is off,
        # which the host key below tells apart.
        return None
    if "is not a member" in out:
        return False
    if "is a member" in out:
        return True
    return None


# FROM PATH, UNLIKE SSH_KEYGEN: the fingerprint is what a person trusts the
# machine on, and the name only says where to look for it, so a false name
# fails at Trust. The Mac app's own command is the fallback, since the app
# puts nothing on PATH until its CLI is installed from its settings.
TAILSCALE_APP_CLI = "/Applications/Tailscale.app/Contents/MacOS/Tailscale"


def tailscale_candidates(which=shutil.which) -> list[str]:
    found = which("tailscale")
    return [p for p in (found, TAILSCALE_APP_CLI) if p]


def tailnet_name(run=_run_quiet, candidates=tailscale_candidates) -> str | None:
    """This machine's MagicDNS name on its Tailscale network, or None
    when Tailscale is absent, stopped, or has MagicDNS off. **A NAME, NEVER A
    100.x ADDRESS:** moving a Mac's Tailscale from the app to the system
    service registers it as a new device with a new address, and a Deck host
    entry holding the old one stops connecting, while the name
    follows the machine."""
    for path in candidates():
        if not os.access(path, os.X_OK):
            continue
        code, out = run([path, "status", "--json"])
        if code != 0:
            continue
        status = json.loads(out)
        if status.get("BackendState") != "Running":
            return None
        # Self.DNSName is the node's name whether or not MagicDNS is on, and
        # with it off the other computer cannot resolve that name.
        if not (status.get("CurrentTailnet") or {}).get("MagicDNSEnabled"):
            return None
        name = ((status.get("Self") or {}).get("DNSName") or "").rstrip(".")
        return name or None
    return None


def pairing_lines(user: str, host_key: Path = HOST_KEY, run=_run_quiet,
                  allows=ssh_allows, name=tailnet_name) -> list[str]:
    """What Deck's Add Host needs from this machine. **THE FINGERPRINT IS THE
    ONE DECK SHOWS AT TRUST**, in the SHA256 form `ssh-keygen -l` prints, so
    the person compares two strings rather than trusting the first answer a
    network gives. Read from the public host key, which every account may
    read, so this needs no administrator."""
    if not host_key.is_file():
        how = ("turn on Remote Login in System Settings › General › Sharing"
               if sys.platform == "darwin" else
               "install and start the ssh server, the openssh-server package")
        return [
            "THIS MACHINE HAS NO SSH HOST KEY, so it accepts no ssh connections and "
            f"Deck on another computer cannot reach it. Ask whoever administers it to {how}.",
        ]
    code, out = run([SSH_KEYGEN, "-lf", str(host_key)])
    fields = out.split()
    fingerprint = fields[1] if code == 0 and len(fields) > 1 else None
    if fingerprint is None:
        return [f"could not read the host key fingerprint from {host_key}."]
    address = name()
    if address:
        lines = [
            "To work from Deck on another computer, add this machine in Deck › Settings › "
            "Hosts by its Tailscale name and this account:",
            f"    address: {address}",
        ]
    else:
        lines = [
            "To work from Deck on another computer, add this machine in Deck › Settings › "
            "Hosts, with the name that computer reaches it by and this account. Use a "
            "name rather than a numeric address, which can change when the machine joins "
            "its network again:",
            "    address: <the name that computer reaches it by>",
        ]
    lines += [
        f"    user: {user}",
        "When Deck asks you to trust the machine, check that it shows exactly:",
        f"    {fingerprint} (ED25519)",
    ]
    if allows(user) is False:
        lines += [
            f"REMOTE LOGIN DOES NOT ALLOW {user} YET, so Deck will be refused. Ask "
            "whoever administers this Mac to add the account under System Settings › "
            "General › Sharing › Remote Login › Allow access for.",
        ]
    return lines


def closing_pairing(euid: int, user: str, shape: str, lines=pairing_lines) -> list[str]:
    """The pairing block, NEVER AS ROOT: the account named is the one Deck
    logs in as, and under sudo it would be root, followed by advice to let
    root in over ssh. Root gets one line saying where pairing comes from."""
    if euid == 0:
        return ["Pairing with Deck is not shown when this runs as root. Run "
                f"`{PROG} --shape {shape} --status` as the account Deck will log in "
                "as, to see the host key fingerprint it should trust."]
    return lines(user)


# --- the migration: --migrate-from and --rollback-to --------------------------
#
# **THE MIGRATION IS PLANNED FIRST, THEN RUN, AND RECORDED AS IT RUNS.** The plan
# reads the operator's files and lists every move, copy, rewrite and ownership
# change before anything changes, so the refusals and the dry run describe what
# would really happen. Each path a step is about to change is recorded as a source
# and a destination BEFORE the step runs, so a run that stops half way has written
# down every place it may have touched. Rollback reverses those pairs in reverse
# order, and derives each reversal from the pair and from what is on disk. Nothing
# is reversed from memory, and no operation or command is stored.
#
# **EVERY FILE CHANGE IS MADE IN PYTHON AS ROOT, THROUGH DIRECTORY HANDLES.** The
# `agents` account writes all of its own home, so any destination in it can be made
# a symlink before root arrives. Each component from a home down is opened with
# O_NOFOLLOW from the descriptor of the one above it, and each entry root creates is
# opened and handed to its owner through that descriptor. No shell command makes a
# file change: `mv`, `cp`, `tee` and `chown -R` each resolve their path again, and a
# symlink swapped in between would be followed as root.
#
# **THE RECORD IS ROOT'S.** It sits under the root-owned prefix, and rollback reads it
# only when it and every directory down to it are owned by root with no group or other
# write bit. `agents` cannot change what rollback will reverse.

RECORD = PRIVILEGED_PREFIX / "var" / "migration.json"
# What moves whole, from the operator's home into `agents`' home. The Builder root is
# not in this list: it is found with the operator's own `builder-root` and moves to the
# same place relative to the `agents` home.
MOVED = (".library", ".orient-control")
# What is copied rather than moved, because the operator keeps a working copy.
COPIED = (".config/convivy",)
# The root pin the session agent reads, inside the copied config directory. It is
# written after the copy, so the copy leaves it out and the rewrite creates it.
PIN_PATH = (".config", "convivy", "session-agent.env")
# Never copied: the operator's own person token. The design keeps it on the operator's
# side, and the copy excludes it by name.
KEPT_OUT_OF_COPY = ("kb-person.json",)
# The part of the operator's Claude setup the backend's Claude needs. Session history
# under `projects/` is not copied: it is keyed on the old paths.
CLAUDE_ITEMS = ("settings.json", "CLAUDE.md", "settings.local.json", "skills", "commands", "plugins")
# The launchd property lists the agent-platform checkout ships. Those labels are the
# Builder jobs an install puts on the operator's account, and the migration refuses
# while any of them is loaded.
AGENT_PLATFORM_LAUNCHD = ("agent-platform", "launchd")
# Where a product service can name a path: a property list, or a sudoers fragment. Any
# one of them naming the old root keeps the path in use by something Builder does not own.
SERVICE_DIRECTORIES = (
    (Path("/Library/LaunchAgents"), "*.plist"),
    (Path("/Library/LaunchDaemons"), "*.plist"),
    (Path("/etc/sudoers.d"), "*"),
)
REMOTE_PREFIX = "git@github.com:"
HTTPS_PREFIX = "https://github.com/"
DIR_OPEN = os.O_RDONLY | os.O_DIRECTORY | os.O_NOFOLLOW
FILE_OPEN = os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NOFOLLOW
NEW_FILE = os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | os.O_NOFOLLOW
# A new file that is handed over is created readable as well. macOS reopens `/dev/fd/N` with the descriptor's
# own access mode, so the ACL clear, which names the descriptor by that path, cannot open a write-only one.
NEW_FILE_RW = os.O_RDWR | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | os.O_NOFOLLOW


def _walk_owned(home: str, parts: list[str], uid: int) -> int:
    """A descriptor for `home/parts`, each directory opened without following a symlink and owned by `uid`."""
    handle = os.open(home, DIR_OPEN)
    try:
        if os.fstat(handle).st_uid != uid:
            raise Refused(f"refusing {home}: it is not owned by uid {uid}. Nothing was changed.")
        for name in parts:
            child = _child(handle, name, home)
            os.close(handle)
            handle = child
            if os.fstat(handle).st_uid != uid:
                raise Refused(f"refusing {home}/{name}: it is not owned by uid {uid}. Nothing was changed.")
        return handle
    except BaseException:
        os.close(handle)
        raise


def read_owned(home: str, path: str, uid: int) -> str | None:
    """The text of the regular file at `path` under `home`, read as data, or None when it is missing.

    **THIS READS, IT NEVER EXECUTES.** Every directory from `home` down and the file itself are
    opened without following a symlink, and each must belong to `uid`, so a path the operator
    owns is read only while the operator owns it. A path outside `home` is refused.
    """
    if ".." in path.split("/") or not os.path.normpath(path).startswith(home + os.sep):
        raise Refused(f"refusing {path}: it is not under {home}. Nothing was changed.")
    parts = os.path.relpath(path, home).split(os.sep)
    try:
        dir_fd = _walk_owned(home, parts[:-1], uid)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None
    try:
        fd = os.open(parts[-1], FILE_OPEN | os.O_NONBLOCK, dir_fd=dir_fd)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None
    except OSError as exc:
        raise Refused(f"refusing {path}: a symlink is in the way ({exc.strerror}). Nothing was changed.") from exc
    finally:
        os.close(dir_fd)
    try:
        st = os.fstat(fd)
        if not stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode) or st.st_uid != uid:
            raise Refused(f"refusing {path}: it is not a regular file owned by uid {uid}. Nothing was changed.")
        with os.fdopen(fd, "r", encoding="utf-8", closefd=False) as stream:
            return stream.read()
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


def operator_root(op: str, uid: int | None = None) -> str:
    """The Builder root the operator's settings name, read as data and confirmed by the root's own marker.

    **ROOT READS THE ROOT AND NEVER RUNS `builder-root`.** The resolver takes the root from
    `CREWS_ROOT` in the session-agent settings, the pinned file the migration rewrites, and
    refuses a root whose `.builder-root` names another path. This reads the same two lines, with
    every path component owned by the operator and opened without following a symlink. The root
    has no fixed name, so one outside the operator's home has no destination and is refused.
    """
    uid = os.lstat(op).st_uid if uid is None else uid
    settings = read_owned(op, os.path.join(op, *PIN_PATH), uid) or ""
    value = next((m.group(1) for m in (re.match(r"^\s*CREWS_ROOT\s*=\s*(.*?)\s*$", line)
                                       for line in settings.splitlines()) if m), None)
    if value is None or not os.path.isabs(value) or "\n" in value:
        raise Refused(f"the operator's settings at {os.path.join(op, *PIN_PATH)} name no absolute "
                      f"CREWS_ROOT, so there is no Builder root to move. Nothing was changed.")
    if os.path.commonpath([op, value]) != op or value == op:
        raise Refused(f"the Builder root {value} is not inside the operator's home {op}, so the "
                      f"migration has no place to move it to. Nothing was changed.")
    marker = read_owned(op, os.path.join(value, ".builder-root"), uid)
    if marker is None or marker.splitlines()[:1] != [value]:
        raise Refused(f"the Builder root {value} does not name itself in its marker, so the resolver "
                      f"would not trust it either. Nothing was changed.")
    return value


def operator_drop(op: str) -> dict:
    """Keyword arguments that run a child as the operator, dropped, when this process is root. Empty otherwise.

    **GIT READS THE OPERATOR'S CHECKOUTS AND THE OPERATOR'S CONFIGURATION, SO IT RUNS AS THE
    OPERATOR THAT OWNS THEM,** with the operator's own groups and an environment whose home is the
    operator's, never root's. Dropped before the program starts, so nothing in it is ever root's.
    """
    if os.geteuid() != 0:
        return {}
    ident = operator_identity(op)
    groups = os.getgrouplist(ident["account"], ident["gid"])
    return {"user": ident["uid"], "group": ident["gid"], "extra_groups": groups,
            "env": {"PATH": "/usr/bin:/bin", "HOME": op, "LC_ALL": "C"}}


def _operator_git(op: str, argv: list[str], drop: dict, timeout: int = 30):
    """Run one git command for a checkout, dropped to the operator when root runs it, or refused by name."""
    try:
        return subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False, timeout=timeout, **drop)
    except OSError as exc:
        raise Refused(f"could not run git for the checkouts under {op} as the operator who owns them "
                      f"({exc.strerror}). Nothing was changed.") from exc


def git_remotes(root: str, op: str) -> dict[str, str]:
    """Each checkout under `root` whose `origin` is ssh, and that URL. Read as the operator, before anything moves."""
    found = {}
    drop = operator_drop(op)
    for child in sorted(Path(root).iterdir()) if Path(root).is_dir() else []:
        if not (child / ".git").exists():
            continue
        done = _operator_git(op, [GIT, "-C", str(child), "remote", "get-url", "origin"], drop)
        url = done.stdout.strip()
        if done.returncode == 0 and url.startswith(REMOTE_PREFIX):
            found[child.name] = url
    return found


def https_of(ssh_url: str) -> str:
    """The https form of an ssh GitHub URL, which keeps its organisation and repository."""
    return HTTPS_PREFIX + ssh_url[len(REMOTE_PREFIX):].removesuffix(".git")


def operator_identity(op: str) -> dict:
    """The operator's account and primary group, from the directory service.

    **THE ACCOUNT IS THE OWNER OF ITS HOME.** The home was made for the operator, so the
    owner of the operator's home is the operator, and its primary group is read from the
    directory service rather than inferred from the home's name.
    """
    entry = pwd.getpwuid(os.lstat(op).st_uid)
    return {"account": entry.pw_name, "group": grp.getgrgid(entry.pw_gid).gr_name,
            "uid": entry.pw_uid, "gid": entry.pw_gid}


def agents_ids() -> tuple[int, int]:
    try:
        entry = pwd.getpwnam(AGENTS)
    except KeyError:
        raise Refused(f"the `{AGENTS}` account does not exist. Run "
                      f"`sudo bin/provision-accounts`. Nothing was changed.")
    return entry.pw_uid, entry.pw_gid


def plan_migration(op: str, ag: str, *, root: str, labels=(), read=None,
                   exists=os.path.lexists, remotes=None) -> list[dict]:
    """Every change the migration makes, in order. Nothing here changes a file.

    `op` is the operator's home and `ag` is `agents`' home, both as the directory
    service reports them, and `root` is the operator's Builder root under `op`.
    The root lands at the same place relative to `ag`. Files are read as data, owned
    by the operator, and checkouts are listed as the operator.
    """
    read = read or (lambda path: read_owned(op, path, os.lstat(op).st_uid))
    remotes = remotes or (lambda path: git_remotes(path, op))
    op_root = root
    ag_root = os.path.join(ag, os.path.relpath(root, op))
    body: list[dict] = []
    for name in MOVED:
        if exists(f"{op}/{name}"):
            body.append({"op": "move", "src": f"{op}/{name}", "dst": f"{ag}/{name}"})
            body.append({"op": "own", "path": f"{ag}/{name}"})
    # `root` marks the old root's own move: rollback expects a link at its source, not nothing.
    body.append({"op": "move", "src": op_root, "dst": ag_root, "root": True})
    # The operator's old root becomes a link to the moved one. It changes no permission:
    # the moved tree is writable by `agents` at its real path too.
    body.append({"op": "symlink", "target": ag_root, "link": op_root})
    body.append({"op": "own", "path": ag_root})
    # The marker the indexer compares. It is inside the moved root, so it is rewritten there.
    marker = read(f"{op_root}/.builder-root")
    if marker is not None:
        body.append({"op": "rewrite", "path": f"{ag_root}/.builder-root",
                     "text": marker.replace(op_root, ag_root)})
    if exists(f"{op}/{COPIED[0]}"):
        body.append({"op": "copy", "src": f"{op}/{COPIED[0]}", "dst": f"{ag}/{COPIED[0]}",
                     "exclude": [*KEPT_OUT_OF_COPY, PIN_PATH[-1]]})
    pin = read(os.path.join(op, *PIN_PATH))
    if pin is not None:
        body.append({"op": "write", "src": os.path.join(op, *PIN_PATH),
                     "dst": os.path.join(ag, *PIN_PATH),
                     "text": pin.replace(f"CREWS_ROOT={op_root}", f"CREWS_ROOT={ag_root}")})
    for item in CLAUDE_ITEMS:
        if not exists(f"{op}/.claude/{item}"):
            continue
        if item == "settings.json":
            # Hook and skill paths in the settings name the old home, so the copy is rewritten.
            original = read(f"{op}/.claude/{item}") or ""
            body.append({"op": "write", "src": f"{op}/.claude/{item}",
                         "dst": f"{ag}/.claude/{item}", "text": original.replace(op, ag)})
        else:
            body.append({"op": "copy", "src": f"{op}/.claude/{item}",
                         "dst": f"{ag}/.claude/{item}", "exclude": []})
    # The operator's claude.json names the old home in its MCP entries and project trust.
    if exists(f"{op}/.claude.json"):
        text = read(f"{op}/.claude.json") or ""
        body.append({"op": "write", "src": f"{op}/.claude.json", "dst": f"{ag}/.claude.json",
                     "text": text.replace(op, ag)})
    for repo, url in sorted(remotes(op_root).items()):
        body.append({"op": "remote", "repo": f"{ag_root}/{repo}",
                     "before": url, "after": https_of(url)})
    body.append({"op": "daemons", "labels": list(labels)})
    # Containers the migration makes in `agents`' home, outermost first, each one before
    # anything inside it. Only the ones that do not exist yet are made, and each is recorded.
    # A directory another step creates is not a container: the copy of `.config/convivy` makes
    # it, and the rewrite of the pin inside it only writes into it.
    made = {e["dst"] for e in body if e["op"] in ("move", "copy")}
    containers: list[str] = []
    for entry in body:
        if "dst" not in entry:
            continue
        parts = os.path.relpath(entry["dst"], ag).split(os.sep)[:-1]
        for depth in range(1, len(parts) + 1):
            path = os.path.join(ag, *parts[:depth])
            if not exists(path) and path not in containers and path not in made:
                containers.append(path)
    return [{"op": "mkdir", "path": path} for path in containers] + body


def acl_clear_argv(fd: int) -> list[str]:
    """The command that removes every extended ACL entry from the file `fd` holds.

    The command names the descriptor, not a path, so it acts on the inode the caller checked.
    `-h` on macOS keeps the command from following a link; the descriptor is never a link.
    """
    if sys.platform == "darwin":
        return ["/bin/chmod", "-h", "-N", f"/dev/fd/{fd}"]
    return ["/usr/bin/setfacl", "-b", f"/proc/self/fd/{fd}"]


class RootFiles:
    """Owner and ACL changes made as root, each on an open descriptor the caller has checked.

    `owners` maps a role (`operator`, `agents` or `root`) to the (uid, gid) that role's files belong to.
    `run` runs the one command this class needs, which is the ACL clear; the suite injects a fake.
    """

    def __init__(self, owners: dict, run=subprocess.run):
        self.owners = owners
        self.run = run

    def fchown(self, fd: int, who: str, label: str = "") -> None:
        os.fchown(fd, *self.owners[who])

    def clear_acl(self, fd: int, shown: str) -> None:
        """Remove the extended ACL entries of the open entry. A failure refuses, naming the entry.

        A file with no ACL clears with exit 0, so a non-zero exit is a real failure: the tool is
        missing, or the entry could not be changed. The entry is then not handed to anyone.
        """
        done = self.run(acl_clear_argv(fd), pass_fds=(fd,), capture_output=True, text=True,
                        check=False, timeout=60)
        if done.returncode != 0:
            raise Refused(f"refusing {shown}: its access control list could not be cleared, so an "
                          f"account granted access to it would keep that access (exit {done.returncode}: "
                          f"{(done.stderr or done.stdout).strip()[-200:]}). Its owner had already changed "
                          f"when this ran. The record says what did complete.")


def _cleared(argv, **kwargs) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
    """The default answer for a fixture: the ACL clear succeeded."""
    return subprocess.CompletedProcess(argv, 0, stdout="", stderr="")


class FixtureFiles(RootFiles):
    """RootFiles on a fixture. The suite is not root, so no owner is changed here.

    Each owner the migration would set is compared with the one `expect` gives, and each
    comparison is kept. A wrong owner or group is therefore a failure the test reads, not
    a call that was merely made. ACL clears are recorded in `cleared` and sent to `run`,
    which the suite answers; by default every clear succeeds.
    """

    def __init__(self, owners: dict, expect: dict, run=None):
        super().__init__(owners, run=run or _cleared)
        self.expect = expect
        self.seen: list[str] = []
        self.wrong: list[str] = []
        self.cleared: list[str] = []
        # Every owner change and ACL clear, in the order they were made: "chown" or "acl", then the label.
        self.order: list[tuple[str, str]] = []

    def fchown(self, fd: int, who: str, label: str = "") -> None:
        self.order.append(("chown", label or "(an entry made by the migration)"))
        self._check(who, label or "(an entry made by the migration)")

    def clear_acl(self, fd: int, shown: str) -> None:
        self.order.append(("acl", shown))
        self.cleared.append(shown)
        super().clear_acl(fd, shown)

    def _check(self, who: str, name: str) -> None:
        got, want = tuple(self.owners[who]), tuple(self.expect[who])
        self.seen.append(f"{name} as {who}")
        if got != want:
            self.wrong.append(f"{name} as {who}: would set {got}, the fixture expects {want}")


def _open_dir_at(parent: int, name: str, shown: str) -> int:
    try:
        return os.open(name, DIR_OPEN, dir_fd=parent)
    except OSError as exc:
        raise Refused(f"refusing {shown}: it is a symlink, or a file where a directory belongs "
                      f"({exc.strerror}). The record says what did complete.") from exc


def _child(parent: int, name: str, shown: str) -> int:
    """`name` opened under `parent` with O_NOFOLLOW. A missing component raises FileNotFoundError."""
    try:
        return os.open(name, DIR_OPEN, dir_fd=parent)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        raise
    except OSError as exc:
        raise Refused(f"refusing {shown}: a symlink or a file is in the way ({exc.strerror}). "
                      f"The record says what did complete.") from exc


def _walk(home: str, parts: list[str], shown: str) -> int:
    """A descriptor for `home/parts`, each component opened with O_NOFOLLOW from the one above it."""
    handle = os.open(os.path.realpath(home), DIR_OPEN)
    try:
        for name in parts:
            child = _child(handle, name, shown)
            os.close(handle)
            handle = child
        return handle
    except BaseException:
        os.close(handle)
        raise


def _anchor(home: str, path: str) -> tuple[int, str]:
    """(a descriptor for the directory holding `path`, and the name `path` has there).

    `path` must sit under `home` and must not contain `..`. The caller closes the descriptor.
    """
    if ".." in path.split("/"):
        raise Refused(f"refusing {path}: it contains `..`, so it is not a path under {home}.")
    rel = os.path.relpath(path, home)
    if rel in (os.curdir, os.pardir) or rel.startswith(os.pardir + os.sep) or os.path.isabs(rel):
        raise Refused(f"refusing {path}: it is not under {home}.")
    parts = rel.split(os.sep)
    return _walk(home, parts[:-1], path), parts[-1]


@contextlib.contextmanager
def _held(home: str, path: str):
    fd, name = _anchor(home, path)
    try:
        yield fd, name
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


def _entry_at(parent: int, name: str):
    try:
        return os.stat(name, dir_fd=parent, follow_symlinks=False)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None


def _write_new(parent: int, name: str, text: str, shown: str, files, who: str) -> None:
    """Create `name` with `text` at mode 600, refusing if anything is there, and hand it to `who`."""
    try:
        fd = os.open(name, NEW_FILE_RW, 0o600, dir_fd=parent)
    except OSError as exc:
        raise Refused(f"could not create {shown}: {exc.strerror}. The record says what did complete.") from exc
    try:
        _hand_to(fd, who, files, shown)
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w", encoding="utf-8", closefd=False) as stream:
            stream.write(text)
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


MARKER_LIMIT = 4096  # a marker is one path line; anything longer is refused, not read into memory


def _read_marker(moved: int, shown: str) -> str | None:
    """The text of `.builder-root` in the moved tree at `moved`, read as data, or None when there is none.

    **OPENED WITHOUT FOLLOWING A LINK AND WITHOUT BLOCKING ON A FIFO, IT MUST BE A REGULAR FILE, AND
    IT IS READ AT MOST `MARKER_LIMIT` BYTES.** Anything else is refused by name, so a marker an agent
    replaced is never read or unlinked around, and a large one never fills root's memory.
    """
    if _entry_at(moved, ".builder-root") is None:
        return None
    try:
        fd = os.open(".builder-root", FILE_OPEN | os.O_NONBLOCK, dir_fd=moved)
    except OSError as exc:
        raise Refused(f"refusing {shown}/.builder-root: it is a symlink, or it changed while it was "
                      f"read ({exc.strerror}).") from exc
    try:
        if not stat.S_ISREG(os.fstat(fd).st_mode):
            raise Refused(f"refusing {shown}/.builder-root: it is not a regular file.")
        # Bytes are counted, not characters: a marker of two-byte characters is twice its length in characters.
        chunks, total = [], 0
        while total <= MARKER_LIMIT:
            part = os.read(fd, MARKER_LIMIT + 1 - total)
            if not part:
                break
            chunks.append(part)
            total += len(part)
    finally:
        os.close(fd)
    if total > MARKER_LIMIT:
        raise Refused(f"refusing {shown}/.builder-root: it is larger than {MARKER_LIMIT} bytes, and a "
                      f"marker is one path line.")
    try:
        return b"".join(chunks).decode("utf-8")
    except UnicodeDecodeError as exc:
        raise Refused(f"refusing {shown}/.builder-root: it is not text.") from exc


def _moved_marker(ag: str, dst: str) -> str | None:
    """The marker text of the moved tree at `dst` under `ag`, read through descriptors. Read only."""
    parent, name = _anchor(ag, dst)
    try:
        moved = _open_dir_at(parent, name, dst)
        try:
            return _read_marker(moved, dst)
        finally:
            os.close(moved)
    finally:
        os.close(parent)


def _replace_at(parent: int, name: str, text: str, shown: str, files, who: str) -> None:
    """Replace `name` with a new file holding `text`, without removing the old one first.

    **THE NEW FILE IS WRITTEN UNDER A TEMPORARY NAME AND RENAMED OVER `name`.** A failed write leaves
    the old entry where it was, and the temporary name is removed on failure, so no failure leaves
    `name` missing. The temporary name is refused if it already exists, since it is not ours to remove.
    """
    temp = f".{name}.new"
    try:
        fd = os.open(temp, NEW_FILE_RW, 0o600, dir_fd=parent)
    except OSError as exc:
        raise Refused(f"could not create {shown}/{temp}: {exc.strerror}. Nothing was changed there.") from exc
    try:
        try:
            # The new file is created inside its directory, so it inherits that directory's ACL: the handover clears it.
            _hand_to(fd, who, files, shown)
            with os.fdopen(fd, "w", encoding="utf-8", closefd=False) as stream:
                stream.write(text)
        finally:
            os.close(fd)
        os.rename(temp, name, src_dir_fd=parent, dst_dir_fd=parent)
    except BaseException:
        with contextlib.suppress(FileNotFoundError):
            os.unlink(temp, dir_fd=parent)
        raise


def _private(mode: os.stat_result) -> int:
    """The mode a copy keeps: the source's owner bits only. Group and other bits never reach a copy."""
    return stat.S_IMODE(mode.st_mode) & 0o700


def _open_entry(parent: int, name: str, shown: str) -> int:
    """`name` under `parent`, opened without following a symlink. A symlink, or an entry swapped in since it was looked at, is refused."""
    try:
        return os.open(name, FILE_OPEN | os.O_NONBLOCK, dir_fd=parent)
    except OSError as exc:
        raise Refused(f"refusing {shown}: it is a symlink, or it changed while it was being handled "
                      f"({exc.strerror}). The record says what did complete.") from exc


def _identity(st: os.stat_result) -> tuple[int, int]:
    return st.st_dev, st.st_ino


def _moved_identity(parent: int, name: str, was: tuple[int, int], shown: str) -> tuple[int, int]:
    """Confirm that `name` now holds the inode the move took from its source, and return that identity.

    A rename keeps the inode, so the destination must be that inode. Anything else at the name means
    the entry was swapped after the move, and it is refused.
    """
    fd = _open_entry(parent, name, shown)
    try:
        now = _identity(os.fstat(fd))
    finally:
        os.close(fd)
    if now != was:
        raise Refused(f"refusing {shown}: the entry there is not the one the move took, so it was "
                      f"swapped after the move. The record says what did complete.")
    return now


def _create_dir(parent: int, name: str, shown: str, files, who: str) -> int:
    """Make a directory under `parent`, and return an open descriptor for the directory it made.

    The descriptor is checked against the identity of the directory that was just created, so a
    directory swapped in between is refused rather than handed to `who`.
    """
    try:
        os.mkdir(name, 0o700, dir_fd=parent)
    except OSError as exc:
        raise Refused(f"could not create {shown}: {exc.strerror}. The record says what did complete.") from exc
    made = _identity(os.stat(name, dir_fd=parent, follow_symlinks=False))
    fd = _open_entry(parent, name, shown)
    now = os.fstat(fd)
    if _identity(now) != made or not stat.S_ISDIR(now.st_mode):
        os.close(fd)
        raise Refused(f"refusing {shown}: it was replaced after it was made. The record says what did complete.")
    _hand_to(fd, who, files, shown)
    return fd


def _copy_entry(src: int, dst: int, name: str, exclude, files, who: str) -> None:
    """Copy one entry, and everything under it, from `src` to `dst`, following no symlink.

    Every entry is created through its parent descriptor, opened without following, and
    handed to `who` by descriptor. A name in `exclude` is left out at any depth. A copy keeps
    the source's owner bits and loses the group and other bits, so no other account can read it.
    """
    if name in exclude:
        return
    mode = os.stat(name, dir_fd=src, follow_symlinks=False)
    if stat.S_ISLNK(mode.st_mode):
        # A symlink keeps the owner it was made with: root's, here. Its owner decides no access,
        # and changing it by name could reach the entry a swap put there.
        os.symlink(os.readlink(name, dir_fd=src), name, dir_fd=dst)
    elif stat.S_ISDIR(mode.st_mode):
        made = _create_dir(dst, name, name, files, who)
        try:
            held = _open_dir_at(src, name, name)
            try:
                for child in os.listdir(held):
                    _copy_entry(held, made, child, exclude, files, who)
            finally:
                os.close(held)
            os.fchmod(made, _private(mode))
        finally:
            os.close(made)
    elif stat.S_ISREG(mode.st_mode):
        in_fd = os.open(name, FILE_OPEN, dir_fd=src)
        try:
            out_fd = os.open(name, NEW_FILE_RW, 0o600, dir_fd=dst)
            try:
                _hand_to(out_fd, who, files, name)
                with os.fdopen(in_fd, "rb", closefd=False) as source, \
                        os.fdopen(out_fd, "wb", closefd=False) as target:
                    shutil.copyfileobj(source, target)
                os.fchmod(out_fd, _private(mode))
            finally:
                os.close(out_fd)
        finally:
            os.close(in_fd)


def _hand_to(fd: int, to: str, files, shown: str) -> None:
    """Hand one open entry to `to`: the owner changes first, then the ACL is cleared, then the group and other write bits and the set-id and sticky bits go.

    **THE OWNER CHANGES BEFORE THE ACL IS CLEARED.** Once the owner has moved, the account that held the
    entry is no longer its owner, so a grant that account adds afterwards is one the clear removes. Clearing
    first would leave a gap in which that account could add a grant that the owner change would keep. This
    runs on every handover, in either direction, since an ACL is a grant the owner bits do not show. A clear
    that fails refuses the entry, naming it.

    **A GRANT THAT `writesecurity` OR `chown` GIVES THE ACCOUNT THAT HELD THE ENTRY CAN ACT DURING THE
    WINDOW BETWEEN THE OWNER CHANGE AND THE CLEAR.** That is safe for a handover to the operator. The clear
    removes every ACL entry, and `_not_owned_by` refuses the tree when any entry is not owned by the operator.

    The modes the agents account set are not recorded, so none is restored: the entry keeps its owner
    bits, and no account other than its owner keeps write access to it. A set-id bit is removed here too,
    so an entry the agents account made set-id does not come back runnable as the operator.
    """
    files.fchown(fd, to, shown)
    files.clear_acl(fd, shown)
    os.fchmod(fd, stat.S_IMODE(os.fstat(fd).st_mode)
              & ~(stat.S_IWGRP | stat.S_IWOTH | stat.S_ISUID | stat.S_ISGID | stat.S_ISVTX))


def _reown(parent: int, name: str, *, frm: str, to: str, files, skipped: list[str], shown: str,
           expect: tuple[int, int] | None = None) -> None:
    """Hand one entry, and everything under it, from the `frm` owner to `to`. Anything else is skipped and named.

    **EVERY DECISION IS MADE ON THE DESCRIPTOR THE CHANGE IS MADE ON.** The entry is opened without
    following a symlink, and its owner, identity and link count are read from that open file; the
    owner is then changed through the same descriptor. So a name swapped between the look and the
    change reaches nothing. `expect` binds the top entry to the inode the move produced, and refuses
    a swap. A symlink keeps its owner (see `_copy_entry`). A file with more than one link is left
    alone, since its inode is reachable from outside the tree, and so is anything `frm` does not own.
    """
    before = os.stat(name, dir_fd=parent, follow_symlinks=False)
    if stat.S_ISLNK(before.st_mode):
        # A link keeps its owner, and is named when `frm` owns it: a link `frm` made can point anywhere.
        if before.st_uid == files.owners[frm][0]:
            target = os.readlink(name, dir_fd=parent)
            skipped.append(f"{shown} (a symlink owned by uid {before.st_uid}, pointing at {target}; "
                           f"its owner is kept, so review it)")
        return
    fd = _open_entry(parent, name, shown)
    try:
        now = os.fstat(fd)
        if expect is not None and _identity(now) != expect:
            raise Refused(f"refusing {shown}: the entry there is not the one the move produced, so it was "
                          f"swapped after the move. The record says what did complete.")
        if _identity(now) != _identity(before):
            raise Refused(f"refusing {shown}: it changed between being looked at and being opened.")
        # An entry already owned by `to` is not skipped and not named: a directory's children may still be `frm`'s.
        mine = now.st_uid == files.owners[frm][0]
        if not mine and now.st_uid != files.owners[to][0]:
            skipped.append(f"{shown} (owned by uid {now.st_uid}, not {files.owners[frm][0]}, so it is not re-owned)")
            return
        if stat.S_ISDIR(now.st_mode):
            # The directory is handed over before its children are listed. Once it is no longer `frm`'s, `frm`
            # cannot add an entry to it, so the listing sees every child that will ever be there.
            if mine:
                _hand_to(fd, to, files, shown)
            for child in os.listdir(fd):
                _reown(fd, child, frm=frm, to=to, files=files, skipped=skipped, shown=f"{shown}/{child}")
        elif stat.S_ISREG(now.st_mode):
            if not mine:
                return
            if now.st_nlink != 1:
                skipped.append(f"{shown} (has {now.st_nlink} links, so its inode is reachable outside the tree)")
                return
            _hand_to(fd, to, files, shown)
        else:
            skipped.append(f"{shown} (neither a file nor a directory)")
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


class Leftover(Exception):
    """Entries a rollback found that the operator does not own, after the handover.

    Rollback returns 1 and keeps the record. `state` says where the tree is and what its handover
    changed, so the operator can see what a rerun starts from.
    """

    def __init__(self, lines: list[str], state: str = ""):
        super().__init__("; ".join(lines))
        self.lines = lines
        self.state = state


def _replace_linked(parent: int, name: str, fd: int, files, shown: str) -> None:
    """Replace a regular file with more than one link by an operator-owned copy of its content.

    The other links are names outside the tree that reach the same inode, so the inode itself cannot be
    handed over. The copy is made under a temporary name and renamed over the entry, and the old inode
    keeps its other links. A failed copy removes its temporary name and leaves the entry where it was.
    """
    mode = _private(os.fstat(fd))
    temp = f".{name}.copy"
    try:
        out = os.open(temp, NEW_FILE_RW, 0o600, dir_fd=parent)
    except OSError as exc:
        raise Refused(f"could not create a copy of {shown} as {temp}: {exc.strerror}. Nothing was changed there.") from exc
    try:
        try:
            _hand_to(out, "operator", files, shown)
            with os.fdopen(fd, "rb", closefd=False) as source, os.fdopen(out, "wb", closefd=False) as target:
                shutil.copyfileobj(source, target)
            os.fchmod(out, mode)
        finally:
            os.close(out)
        os.rename(temp, name, src_dir_fd=parent, dst_dir_fd=parent)
    except BaseException:
        with contextlib.suppress(FileNotFoundError):
            os.unlink(temp, dir_fd=parent)
        raise


def _take_back(parent: int, name: str, *, agents: int, files, log, shown: str) -> None:
    """Hand one entry of a tree in staging to the operator, removing or replacing what the agents account made.

    **EVERY CHANGE IS MADE IN THE ROOT-ONLY STAGING DIRECTORY, ON THE DESCRIPTOR IT WAS CHECKED THROUGH.**
    The agents account cannot traverse that directory, so no name in the tree is swapped while it is
    handled. A symlink the agents account owns is removed, and named with its target, since its target
    can point anywhere. A regular file with more than one link is replaced by a copy, since its other
    links reach the same inode from outside the tree. Any other regular file the agents account owns is
    handed over. A directory is handed over before its children are listed.

    **EVERY ENTRY OPENED HERE HAS ITS ACL CLEARED, WHATEVER ITS OWNER.** An entry the operator already owns
    can still carry a grant that a handover left behind when its clear failed, so a rerun clears it too.
    An entry the agents account does not own keeps its owner, and `_not_owned_by` names it afterwards. A
    symlink is not opened, so its ACL is not cleared.
    """
    before = os.stat(name, dir_fd=parent, follow_symlinks=False)
    if stat.S_ISLNK(before.st_mode):
        if before.st_uid == agents:
            target = os.readlink(name, dir_fd=parent)
            os.unlink(name, dir_fd=parent)
            log(f"  removed a link the agents account made: {shown} -> {target}")
        return
    fd = _open_entry(parent, name, shown)
    try:
        now = os.fstat(fd)
        if _identity(now) != _identity(before):
            raise Refused(f"refusing {shown}: it changed between being looked at and being opened.")
        if stat.S_ISDIR(now.st_mode):
            if now.st_uid == agents:
                _hand_to(fd, "operator", files, shown)
            else:
                files.clear_acl(fd, shown)
            for child in os.listdir(fd):
                _take_back(fd, child, agents=agents, files=files, log=log, shown=f"{shown}/{child}")
        elif stat.S_ISREG(now.st_mode) and now.st_nlink > 1:
            _replace_linked(parent, name, fd, files, shown)
            log(f"  replaced {shown}, which has {now.st_nlink} links, with an operator-owned copy of its content")
        elif stat.S_ISREG(now.st_mode) and now.st_uid == agents:
            _hand_to(fd, "operator", files, shown)
        else:
            files.clear_acl(fd, shown)
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


def _not_owned_by(parent: int, name: str, uid: int, shown: str, found: list[str]) -> None:
    """Name each entry under `parent/name` that `uid` does not own, following no symlink. Read only."""
    st = os.stat(name, dir_fd=parent, follow_symlinks=False)
    if st.st_uid != uid:
        found.append(f"{shown} (owned by uid {st.st_uid})")
    if stat.S_ISDIR(st.st_mode):
        held = _open_dir_at(parent, name, shown)
        try:
            for child in os.listdir(held):
                _not_owned_by(held, child, uid, f"{shown}/{child}", found)
        finally:
            os.close(held)


def _return_to_source(stage_fd: int, name: str, home_fd: int, shown: str, staged: str, log) -> str:
    """Put a tree that is still in staging back under its source name, if that name is free. Returns where the tree is.

    Called on every refusal after the move into staging, so the record and the tree stay in the state
    a rerun expects. If the name is taken, the tree is named and left in staging, at `staged`.
    """
    if _entry_at(home_fd, name) is not None:
        log(f"  left {shown} in the staging directory: something now sits where it was")
        return staged
    try:
        os.rename(name, name, src_dir_fd=stage_fd, dst_dir_fd=home_fd)
    except OSError as exc:
        log(f"  left {shown} in the staging directory ({exc.strerror})")
        return staged
    log(f"  returned {shown} from the staging directory; the record is kept")
    return shown


def _remove_tree(parent: int, name: str) -> None:
    """Remove an entry, and everything under it, without following any symlink."""
    if not stat.S_ISDIR(os.stat(name, dir_fd=parent, follow_symlinks=False).st_mode):
        os.unlink(name, dir_fd=parent)
        return
    held = _open_dir_at(parent, name, name)
    try:
        for child in os.listdir(held):
            _remove_tree(held, child)
    finally:
        os.close(held)
    os.rmdir(name, dir_fd=parent)


def pair_of(entry: dict) -> dict | None:
    """The record's entry for one planned step, or None if it keeps none.

    A move records `left`, what its source holds once the migration is done: `symlink` for the
    old root, which the migration links to the moved tree, and `nothing` for every other move.
    """
    kind = entry["op"]
    if kind == "move":
        return {"op": kind, "src": entry["src"], "dst": entry["dst"],
                "left": "symlink" if entry.get("root") else "nothing"}
    if kind in ("copy", "write"):
        return {"op": kind, "src": entry["src"], "dst": entry["dst"]}
    if kind == "mkdir":
        return {"op": kind, "src": "", "dst": entry["path"]}
    if kind == "remote":
        return {"op": kind, "src": entry["before"], "dst": entry["after"]}
    return None


class SudoExecutor:
    """The commands the migration runs as another account, or as root, through sudo.

    **THE FILE CHANGES ARE NOT HERE.** They are made in Python by RootFiles. What is left
    is what sudo has to run: `git` as `agents`, root's copy of supervision, and launchctl.
    `run` and `quiet` are the injection points the suite uses to answer those commands.
    """

    def __init__(self, args=None, run=subprocess.run, quiet=None):
        self.args = args
        self.run = run
        self.quiet = quiet or _run_quiet

    def _must(self, argv: list[str], stdin=None) -> None:
        done = self.run(argv, input=stdin, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False, timeout=600)
        if done.returncode != 0:
            raise Refused(f"`{' '.join(argv[:4])}…` exited {done.returncode}: "
                          f"{(done.stderr or done.stdout).strip()[-400:]}. "
                          f"The record keeps what did complete.")

    def remote(self, repo: str, url: str) -> None:
        self._must([*AS_AGENTS, GIT, "-C", repo, "remote", "set-url", "origin", url])

    def read_remote(self, repo: str) -> str | None:
        done = self.run([*AS_AGENTS, GIT, "-C", repo, "remote", "get-url", "origin"],
                        capture_output=True, text=True, check=False, timeout=60)
        return done.stdout.strip() if done.returncode == 0 else None

    def daemons(self, labels) -> None:
        # Root's copy renders the declarations and bootstraps them, as a full run does.
        full, _shown = root_supervise(self.args, None, run=self.quiet)
        done = self.run(full, check=False)
        if done.returncode != 0:
            raise Refused(f"bootstrapping the daemons exited {done.returncode}. "
                          f"The record keeps what did complete.")

    def bootout(self, label: str) -> None:
        self.run([SUDO, LAUNCHCTL, "bootout", f"system/{label}"],
                 capture_output=True, check=False, timeout=60)


def apply_entry(entry: dict, *, op: str, ag: str, files, executor, moved: dict | None = None) -> list[str]:
    """Make one planned change. Each one is made through a descriptor, not a path.

    `moved` maps each destination a move made to the identity of the inode it holds, so the `own`
    step that follows re-owns exactly that inode. The returned list names each entry an `own` step
    left alone, one line each; every other step returns none.
    """
    kind = entry["op"]
    moved = {} if moved is None else moved
    if kind == "mkdir":
        with _held(ag, entry["path"]) as (fd, name):
            os.close(_create_dir(fd, name, entry["path"], files, "agents"))
    elif kind == "move":
        with _held(op, entry["src"]) as (src_fd, src_name), \
                _held(ag, entry["dst"]) as (dst_fd, dst_name):
            was = _identity(os.stat(src_name, dir_fd=src_fd, follow_symlinks=False))
            try:
                os.rename(src_name, dst_name, src_dir_fd=src_fd, dst_dir_fd=dst_fd)
            except OSError as exc:
                raise Refused(f"moving {entry['src']} to {entry['dst']} failed: {exc.strerror}. "
                              f"The record keeps what did complete.") from exc
            moved[entry["dst"]] = _moved_identity(dst_fd, dst_name, was, entry["dst"])
    elif kind == "symlink":
        with _held(op, entry["link"]) as (fd, name):
            os.symlink(entry["target"], name, dir_fd=fd)
    elif kind == "own":
        if entry["path"] not in moved:
            raise Refused(f"{entry['path']} was not moved by this migration, so it is not re-owned. "
                          f"The record keeps what did complete.")
        skipped: list[str] = []
        with _held(ag, entry["path"]) as (fd, name):
            _reown(fd, name, frm="operator", to="agents", files=files, skipped=skipped,
                   shown=entry["path"], expect=moved[entry["path"]])
        return skipped
    elif kind == "rewrite":
        with _held(ag, entry["path"]) as (fd, name):
            _replace_at(fd, name, entry["text"], entry["path"], files, "agents")
    elif kind == "write":
        with _held(ag, entry["dst"]) as (fd, name):
            _write_new(fd, name, entry["text"], entry["dst"], files, "agents")
    elif kind == "copy":
        with _held(op, entry["src"]) as (src_fd, src_name), \
                _held(ag, entry["dst"]) as (dst_fd, dst_name):
            if src_name != dst_name:
                raise Refused(f"copying {entry['src']} to {entry['dst']} needs the same name at both ends.")
            _copy_entry(src_fd, dst_fd, src_name, entry["exclude"], files, "agents")
    elif kind == "remote":
        executor.remote(entry["repo"], entry["after"])
    elif kind == "daemons":
        executor.daemons(entry["labels"])
    else:
        raise Refused(f"unknown migration step {kind!r}")
    return []


def _preflight(ops: list[dict], op: str, ag: str) -> list[str]:
    """Every destination whose path already holds something, or passes through a symlink.

    Read only. These are refused before any change, so a symlink planted in `agents`' home
    stops the migration rather than being followed by a step halfway through it.
    """
    reasons = []
    for entry in ops:
        dst = entry.get("dst")
        if dst is None or entry["op"] == "mkdir":
            continue
        if os.path.lexists(dst):
            reasons.append(f"{dst} already exists, and the migration would not overwrite it. "
                           f"Move it aside, then run again.")
        try:
            fd, name = _anchor(ag, dst)
        except FileNotFoundError:
            continue  # a container the migration makes; checked when it is made
        except Refused as exc:
            reasons.append(str(exc))
            continue
        os.close(fd)
    return reasons


def record_refusal(record: Path = RECORD) -> str | None:
    """The refusal for a migration whose record already exists, or None. Checked before anything else in a migration."""
    if os.path.lexists(record):
        return (f"a migration already ran here: {record} exists. Roll it back first, or the second "
                f"run would move a root the first one left behind. Nothing was changed.")
    return None


def migration_refusals(op: str, ag: str, *, root: str, loaded=None,
                       dirty=None, plists=None, declared=()) -> list[str]:
    """Every reason the migration must not run now, one line each. Nothing is changed.

    `loaded(label)` asks the operator's launchd domain; `dirty(root)` lists the
    checkouts with uncommitted work, read as the operator; `plists(op, root, builder)`
    lists product services that name the root about to move. Each one is an injection
    point so the suite can answer from a fixture.
    """
    # The operator's domain is the operator's uid, read from its home: under sudo the process is root.
    operator_uid = os.lstat(op).st_uid
    loaded = loaded or (lambda label: _loaded_in_operator_domain(label, operator_uid))
    dirty = dirty or (lambda path: dirty_checkouts(path, op))
    plists = plists or service_plists_naming
    op_root = root
    reasons = []
    builder_labels = set(declared) | {
        p.stem for p in Path(root, *AGENT_PLATFORM_LAUNCHD).glob("*.plist")}
    running = sorted(label for label in builder_labels if loaded(label))
    if running:
        reasons.append(
            "the operator's session still has these Builder jobs loaded, so they would "
            "keep running from the moved tree: " + ", ".join(running) + ". Boot them "
            "out, as the migration runbook's freeze step says, and run again.")
    unclean = dirty(op_root)
    if unclean:
        reasons.append(
            "these checkouts have uncommitted changes, which a move would leave in "
            "the wrong place: " + "; ".join(f"{repo} ({n} path(s))" for repo, n in unclean)
            + ". Commit or stash them, then run again.")
    services = plists(op, op_root, builder_labels)
    if services:
        reasons.append(
            "these services name the root that is about to move, and each needs its own "
            "checkout outside it first, or its entry removed: " + ", ".join(services) + ".")
    return reasons


# launchctl's exit for a label that is not loaded in the domain it was asked about, as
# `launchctl print gui/<uid>/<absent label>` returns it. It is the only "not loaded".
LAUNCHCTL_ABSENT = 113


def _launchctl_loaded(label: str, domain: str) -> bool:
    """Whether launchctl has `label` loaded in `domain`.

    **ANY EXIT OTHER THAN 0 OR 113 REFUSES, NAMING THE LABEL AND THE STATUS.** A check that reads
    every failure as "not loaded" lets the migration or the rollback proceed while a job still runs.
    """
    try:
        done = subprocess.run([LAUNCHCTL, "print", f"{domain}/{label}"],
                              capture_output=True, check=False, timeout=30)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as exc:
        raise Refused(f"could not ask launchctl whether {label} is loaded in {domain} ({exc}). "
                      f"Refusing, since that is not known.") from exc
    if done.returncode == 0:
        return True
    if done.returncode == LAUNCHCTL_ABSENT:
        return False
    raise Refused(f"launchctl print {domain}/{label} exited {done.returncode}, so whether {label} "
                  f"is loaded is not known. Refusing, since that is not known.")


def _loaded_in_operator_domain(label: str, uid: int) -> bool:
    return _launchctl_loaded(label, f"gui/{uid}")


def _loaded_in_system_domain(label: str) -> bool:
    return _launchctl_loaded(label, "system")


PGREP = "/usr/bin/pgrep"


def agents_processes(uid: int, run=subprocess.run) -> list[str]:
    """Each process that runs as `uid`, as `pid name`, or [] when there is none.

    **ANY EXIT OTHER THAN 0 OR 1 REFUSES, AND ANY LINE THAT IS NOT `pid name` REFUSES.** pgrep exits 1
    when nothing matches. A check that read a failure as "nothing running" would let rollback change a
    tree an agent still writes. Root's own processes are not `uid`'s, so the check never sees the
    rollback itself.
    """
    try:
        done = run([PGREP, "-l", "-U", str(uid)], capture_output=True, text=True, check=False, timeout=30)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as exc:
        raise Refused(f"could not list the processes of uid {uid} ({exc}). Refusing, since that is not "
                      f"known.") from exc
    if done.returncode == 1 and not done.stdout.strip():
        return []
    if done.returncode != 0:
        raise Refused(f"{PGREP} -U {uid} exited {done.returncode}, so the processes that run as uid {uid} are "
                      f"not known. Refusing.")
    lines = done.stdout.splitlines()
    for line in lines:
        if not re.fullmatch(r"\d+ \S.*", line):
            raise Refused(f"{PGREP} printed a line that is not `pid name`: {line!r}. Refusing, since the "
                          f"processes are not known.")
    if not lines:
        raise Refused(f"{PGREP} exited 0 and printed nothing, which is not an answer.")
    return lines


def dirty_checkouts(root: str, op: str) -> list[tuple[str, int]]:
    """Each checkout under `root` with uncommitted paths, and how many. Read as the operator, never as root."""
    found = []
    drop = operator_drop(op)
    for child in sorted(Path(root).iterdir()) if Path(root).is_dir() else []:
        if not (child / ".git").exists():
            continue
        done = _operator_git(op, [GIT, "-C", str(child), "status", "--porcelain"], drop, timeout=120)
        lines = [line for line in done.stdout.splitlines() if line.strip()]
        if done.returncode != 0 or lines:
            found.append((child.name, len(lines) or 1))
    return found


def service_plists_naming(op: str, root: str, builder: set, directories=None) -> list[str]:
    """Product services whose property list or sudoers fragment names the root being moved.

    A property list is named by its label and a sudoers fragment by its file name. A
    Builder job's own list is exempt, because the freeze step boots it out first. The root
    matches as a whole path component, so `Convivy` does not match `Convivy-old`.
    """
    pattern = re.compile(re.escape(root) + r"(?=$|[/\s\"'<:,])", re.M)
    sources = directories or [(Path(op, "Library", "LaunchAgents"), "*.plist"), *SERVICE_DIRECTORIES]
    found = []
    for directory, glob in sources:
        if not directory.is_dir():
            continue
        for entry in sorted(directory.glob(glob)):
            if not entry.is_file():
                continue
            name = entry.stem if glob == "*.plist" else entry.name
            if glob == "*.plist" and name in builder:
                continue
            try:
                text = entry.read_bytes().decode("utf-8", "replace")
            except OSError:
                continue
            if pattern.search(text):
                found.append(name)
    return found


# --- the record: what rollback may reverse, read only through root's directories --

def _trusted_chain(path: Path, owner: int, top: Path) -> None:
    """Refuse unless `path`, and each directory from `top` down to it, is owned by `owner` with no group or other write bit.

    No component may be a symlink. The file itself must also carry no group or other bit.
    """
    chain = [p for p in [*reversed(path.parents), path] if p == top or top in p.parents]
    for part in chain:
        try:
            info = os.lstat(part)
        except FileNotFoundError:
            raise Refused(f"there is no migration record at {path}.") from None
        if stat.S_ISLNK(info.st_mode):
            raise Refused(f"refusing {part}: it is a symlink, and the migration record is read only "
                          f"through directories and a file that root owns.")
        if info.st_uid != owner or info.st_mode & (stat.S_IWGRP | stat.S_IWOTH):
            raise Refused(f"refusing {part}: it is not owned by uid {owner} with no group or other "
                          f"write bit.")
        if part == path and info.st_mode & 0o077:
            raise Refused(f"refusing {path}: the record must be mode 0600, with no group or other "
                          f"bit.")


def read_record(path: Path, owner: int = 0, top: Path = Path("/")) -> dict:
    _trusted_chain(path, owner, top)
    try:
        return json.loads(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:
        raise Refused(f"the migration record at {path} is unreadable ({exc}).") from exc


def store_record(path: Path, data: dict) -> None:
    """Write the record whole: a temporary file made with O_EXCL, then renamed over the record."""
    temp = path.with_name(path.name + ".tmp")
    try:
        fd = os.open(temp, NEW_FILE, 0o600)
    except FileExistsError:
        raise Refused(f"{temp} is left from an earlier run that stopped while writing the record. "
                      f"Remove it, then run again. Nothing was changed by this run.") from None
    try:
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w", encoding="utf-8", closefd=False) as stream:
            stream.write(json.dumps(data, indent=2, sort_keys=True) + "\n")
        os.fsync(fd)
    finally:
        os.close(fd)
    os.rename(temp, path)


def recorded_owner(data: dict) -> tuple[int, int]:
    """The operator's uid and gid as the record names them, checked against the account's own primary group.

    The account and the group are looked up by name in the directory service. A group that is
    not the account's primary group is refused, so a record cannot hand the operator's tree to
    some other group.
    """
    account, group = data.get("operator_account"), data.get("operator_group")
    try:
        entry = pwd.getpwnam(account)
        gid = grp.getgrnam(group).gr_gid
    except (KeyError, TypeError) as exc:
        raise Refused(f"the record names the operator's account {account!r} and group {group!r}, "
                      f"and the directory service does not know them ({exc}).") from exc
    if gid != entry.pw_gid:
        raise Refused(f"the record names group {group}, which is not the primary group of "
                      f"{account}.")
    return entry.pw_uid, gid


def checked_pairs(pairs: list, op: str, ag: str) -> list[dict]:
    """The recorded pairs, each one a path under the operator's home or `agents`' home, with no `..`.

    A remote pair holds two URLs rather than paths, and is checked by its scheme instead.
    """
    for pair in pairs:
        if pair["op"] == "remote":
            continue
        for field, home in (("src", op), ("dst", ag)):
            path = pair[field]
            if path == "" and field == "src":
                continue
            normal = os.path.normpath(path)
            if ".." in path.split("/") or not (normal == home or normal.startswith(home + os.sep)):
                raise Refused(f"the record names {path!r}, which is not under {home}.")
    return pairs


def _entry_status(home: str, path: str):
    """What sits at `path` under `home`, read without following a symlink: its stat, or None when nothing does."""
    try:
        fd, name = _anchor(home, path)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None
    try:
        return _entry_at(fd, name)
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


def _link_target(home: str, path: str) -> str | None:
    """Where the symlink at `path` under `home` points, or None when there is no symlink there."""
    try:
        fd, name = _anchor(home, path)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None
    try:
        st = _entry_at(fd, name)
        if st is None or not stat.S_ISLNK(st.st_mode):
            return None
        return os.readlink(name, dir_fd=fd)
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


def _describe_entry(st: os.stat_result, link_to: str | None) -> str:
    if link_to is not None:
        return f"a link to {link_to}"
    return "a directory" if stat.S_ISDIR(st.st_mode) else "a file"


def plan_undo(op: str, ag: str, pairs: list[dict]) -> list[tuple[dict, str]]:
    """What rollback does for each recorded pair, read from disk before anything changes. Refuses by name.

    **A PAIR IS REVERSED ONLY WHEN ITS SOURCE HOLDS EXACTLY WHAT THE MIGRATION LEFT.** A move's
    source holds nothing, and the old root's source holds the link to the moved tree, or nothing
    when the migration stopped before making it. Anything else there means the moved tree was
    not left where the record says, so it stays where it is and the rollback is refused. A copy or
    a write is undone by removing its copy, which is only safe while the operator's original still
    exists: without it the copy is the only one left. A move's destination is never removed.

    Each action is one of `done` (already reversed), `move`, `link-and-move`, `remove`, `mkdir`
    and `remote`, and rollback runs them in reverse order of the record.
    """
    plan: list[tuple[dict, str]] = []
    for pair in pairs:
        kind = pair["op"]
        if kind in ("mkdir", "remote"):
            plan.append((pair, kind))
            continue
        src, dst = pair["src"], pair["dst"]
        dst_st = _entry_status(ag, dst)
        src_st = _entry_status(op, src)
        if kind == "move":
            link_to = _link_target(op, src)
            if dst_st is not None and stat.S_ISLNK(dst_st.st_mode):
                raise Refused(f"{dst} is a link, not the moved tree the migration made.")
            if dst_st is not None and stat.S_ISDIR(dst_st.st_mode):
                # THE MARKER IS READ HERE, BEFORE ANY LINK IS UNLINKED, SO A MARKER THAT IS NOT A
                # REGULAR FILE REFUSES WHILE THE OPERATOR'S ROOT IS STILL WHERE IT WAS.
                _moved_marker(ag, dst)
            if dst_st is None:
                if src_st is None:
                    raise Refused(f"the moved tree is not at {dst}, and nothing is at {src}.")
                if link_to is not None:
                    raise Refused(f"{src} links to {dst}, which is gone.")
                plan.append((pair, "done"))
            elif src_st is None:
                plan.append((pair, "move"))
            elif pair.get("left") == "symlink" and link_to == dst:
                plan.append((pair, "link-and-move"))
            else:
                raise Refused(f"{src} holds {_describe_entry(src_st, link_to)}, which the migration did not "
                              f"leave there, so the moved tree at {dst} stays where it is.")
        else:
            if src_st is None:
                raise Refused(f"{src} is gone, so the copy at {dst} is the only copy left, and it stays.")
            plan.append((pair, "remove" if dst_st is not None else "done"))
    return plan


def _staging_name() -> str:
    """The name of this run's staging directory in the operator's home."""
    return f".builder-undo-{os.getpid()}"


@contextlib.contextmanager
def _staging(op: str, files, log):
    """A directory in the operator's home, owned by root and mode 0700, that a moved tree passes through.

    `agents` cannot write into it, so a tree in it cannot be swapped between two renames. It is
    removed when the block ends if it is empty. A tree left in it is named, not removed.
    """
    home = _walk(op, [], op)
    name = _staging_name()
    try:
        try:
            os.mkdir(name, 0o700, dir_fd=home)
        except FileExistsError as exc:
            raise Refused(f"{op}/{name} is left from an earlier rollback that stopped. Remove it, then run "
                          f"again.") from exc
        stage = os.open(name, DIR_OPEN, dir_fd=home)
        try:
            # A new directory inherits the operator home's ACL, so the handover clears it: nothing but root may traverse this.
            _hand_to(stage, "root", files, f"{op}/{name}")
            yield stage
        finally:
            os.close(stage)
            try:
                os.rmdir(name, dir_fd=home)
            except OSError:
                log(f"  left {op}/{name}: it still holds the tree named above")
    finally:
        os.close(home)


def _tree_state(state: dict) -> str:
    """What a refusal says about its tree: where the tree is now, and whether its handover had begun."""
    if not state["handing"]:
        return f"The tree is at {state['where']}, and no owner, write bit or ACL on it has changed."
    return (f"The tree is at {state['where']}. Its handover had begun: the entries it reached that the agents "
            f"account owned now have the operator as owner, entries owned by any other account kept their "
            f"owner, and any entry it did not finish may keep a group or other write bit or an ACL. The entries "
            f"it had not reached are as the migration left them.")


def _undo_move(op: str, ag: str, src: str, dst: str, *, unlink: bool, files, log, before_stage=None,
               agents_idle=None) -> None:
    """Move a recorded tree back to its source, and hand it to the operator inside the staging directory.

    **THE TREE NEVER REACHES THE OPERATOR'S PATH UNTIL IT HAS BEEN HANDED OVER.** The order is: confirm the
    identity in `agents`' home; move the tree into the root-only staging directory, which `agents` cannot
    traverse; confirm the identity there; hand the tree over (`_take_back`, then the marker rewrite); check
    that nothing in it is still not the operator's; confirm the identity again; check that no process runs as
    `agents`; unlink the operator's link; and move the tree into place. Every refusal returns the tree to its
    source, or leaves it in staging, and keeps the record. Its message says where the tree is and what the
    handover changed (`_tree_state`), so a rerun starts from a state the operator can read.

    `before_stage(dst)` runs between the first identity check and the move into staging. `agents_idle()`
    runs once the tree is handed over, and raises when a process runs as `agents`. Both exist so the suite
    can act in those windows.
    """
    state = {"where": dst, "handing": False}
    try:
        _undo_move_tree(op, ag, src, dst, unlink=unlink, files=files, log=log, before_stage=before_stage,
                        agents_idle=agents_idle, state=state)
    except Refused as exc:
        raise Refused(f"{exc} {_tree_state(state)}") from exc
    except Leftover as exc:
        raise Leftover(exc.lines, _tree_state(state)) from exc


def _undo_move_tree(op: str, ag: str, src: str, dst: str, *, unlink: bool, files, log, before_stage,
                    agents_idle, state: dict) -> None:
    """The steps of `_undo_move`. `state` is set to where the tree is, and whether its handover has begun, at each step."""
    dst_fd, dst_name = _anchor(ag, dst)
    try:
        dst_st = os.stat(dst_name, dir_fd=dst_fd, follow_symlinks=False)
        was = _identity(dst_st)
        _moved_identity(dst_fd, dst_name, was, dst)
        src_fd, src_name = _anchor(op, src)
        staged = os.path.join(op, _staging_name(), dst_name)
        try:
            with _staging(op, files, log) as stage_fd:
                if before_stage is not None:
                    before_stage(dst)
                try:
                    os.rename(dst_name, dst_name, src_dir_fd=dst_fd, dst_dir_fd=stage_fd)
                except OSError as exc:
                    raise Refused(f"moving {dst} into the staging directory failed: {exc.strerror}. "
                                  f"Nothing was unlinked.") from exc
                state["where"] = staged
                try:
                    _moved_identity(stage_fd, dst_name, was, dst)
                except Refused:
                    # The entry that was swapped in goes back where it came from, unless something now sits there.
                    if _entry_at(dst_fd, dst_name) is None:
                        os.rename(dst_name, dst_name, src_dir_fd=stage_fd, dst_dir_fd=dst_fd)
                        state["where"] = dst
                    raise
                try:
                    state["handing"] = True
                    _take_back(stage_fd, dst_name, agents=files.owners["agents"][0], files=files, log=log, shown=dst)
                    # The marker is read and rewritten through the tree in staging, after the identity is confirmed.
                    tree = _open_dir_at(stage_fd, dst_name, dst)
                    try:
                        marker = _read_marker(tree, dst)
                        if marker is not None:
                            _replace_at(tree, ".builder-root", marker.replace(dst, src), f"{dst}/.builder-root",
                                        files, "operator")
                    finally:
                        os.close(tree)
                    found: list[str] = []
                    _not_owned_by(stage_fd, dst_name, files.owners["operator"][0], dst, found)
                    if found:
                        raise Leftover(found)
                    _moved_identity(stage_fd, dst_name, was, dst)
                    if agents_idle is not None:
                        agents_idle()
                except BaseException:
                    state["where"] = _return_to_source(stage_fd, dst_name, dst_fd, dst, staged, log)
                    raise
                restore = None
                if unlink and _entry_at(src_fd, src_name) is not None:
                    restore = os.readlink(src_name, dir_fd=src_fd)
                    os.unlink(src_name, dir_fd=src_fd)
                    log(f"  undo link: {src}")
                try:
                    os.rename(dst_name, src_name, src_dir_fd=stage_fd, dst_dir_fd=src_fd)
                except OSError as exc:
                    # The link is put back, so the operator's root is where it was, and the tree returns to its source.
                    if restore is not None:
                        os.symlink(restore, src_name, dir_fd=src_fd)
                    state["where"] = _return_to_source(stage_fd, dst_name, dst_fd, dst, staged, log)
                    link = f", and the operator's link at {src} was put back" if restore is not None else ""
                    raise Refused(f"moving {dst} to {src} failed: {exc.strerror}. Nothing was moved into place"
                                  f"{link}.") from exc
                state["where"] = src
                _moved_identity(src_fd, src_name, was, src)
                log(f"  undo move: {dst} -> {src}")
        finally:
            os.close(src_fd)
    finally:
        os.close(dst_fd)


def _undo_copy(ag: str, dst: str, *, log) -> None:
    """Remove a copy the migration made. Only `plan_undo` decides it may be removed."""
    dst_fd, dst_name = _anchor(ag, dst)
    try:
        _remove_tree(dst_fd, dst_name)
    finally:
        os.close(dst_fd)
    log(f"  undo copy: removed {dst}")


def _undo_container(ag: str, dst: str, *, log) -> None:
    """Remove a directory the migration made, if it is empty. A non-empty one holds later work, so it stays."""
    try:
        fd, name = _anchor(ag, dst)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return
    try:
        os.rmdir(name, dir_fd=fd)
        log(f"  undo mkdir: {dst}")
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass
    except OSError as exc:
        log(f"  left {dst}: it holds files written since the migration ({exc.strerror})")
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


def _restore_remote(pair: dict, pairs: list[dict], executor, *, log) -> None:
    """Point a checkout back at the ssh URL the record names, where it still has the https one."""
    for other in pairs:
        if other["op"] == "remote" or other["src"] == "":
            continue
        if not os.path.isdir(other["dst"]) or os.path.islink(other["dst"]):
            continue
        for child in sorted(Path(other["dst"]).iterdir()):
            if (child / ".git").exists() and executor.read_remote(str(child)) == pair["dst"]:
                executor.remote(str(child), pair["src"])
                log(f"  undo remote: {child} origin back to {pair['src']}")


UNCHANGED = ("Nothing was rolled back: no owner, write bit or ACL has changed, and each tree is where the "
             "migration left it.")
BOOTED = UNCHANGED + " The daemons were booted out before this check."
EARLIER = "Steps this run finished before this tree are not undone."


def rollback(op: str, ag: str, *, record: Path = RECORD, owner: int = 0, top: Path = Path("/"),
             files_for=None, executor=None, loaded=None, labels=None, log=print, hooks=None,
             agents_running=None) -> int:
    """Reverse a recorded migration from its record alone. 0 done, 1 not finished, 2 refused.

    Every pair is checked against the disk before anything is changed, so a refusal leaves the
    migrated tree and the record where they were. The daemons are the exception: each declared
    label is booted out before the process and still-loaded checks, so a refusal leaves them booted
    out, and the refusal names each process or daemon still running. `agents_running(uid)` lists
    the processes that run as `agents`, and is checked again just before a tree leaves staging.
    Exit 1 means entries are still not the operator's: the record is kept, and a rerun repeats the
    handover. A refusal says where each tree is and whether its owners, write bits or ACLs changed.
    `hooks` holds the seams the suite uses (`before_stage`).
    """
    hooks = hooks or {}
    agents_running = agents_running or agents_processes
    op, ag = os.path.abspath(op), os.path.abspath(ag)
    try:
        data = read_record(record, owner, top)
        if data.get("operator_home") != op or data.get("agents_home") != ag:
            raise Refused(f"the record at {record} is for {data.get('operator_home')} and "
                          f"{data.get('agents_home')}, not {op} and {ag}.")
        pairs = checked_pairs(data.get("pairs", []), op, ag)
        actions = plan_undo(op, ag, pairs)
        agents_st = os.lstat(ag)
        files = (files_for or RootFiles)({"operator": recorded_owner(data),
                                          "agents": (agents_st.st_uid, agents_st.st_gid),
                                          "root": (0, 0)})
    except Refused as exc:
        raise Refused(f"{exc} {UNCHANGED}") from exc
    except OSError as exc:
        # EVERY CHECK ABOVE RUNS BEFORE THE FIRST CHANGE, SO AN OS ERROR HERE IS A REFUSAL, NOT A TRACEBACK.
        raise Refused(f"could not check {exc.filename or 'the migration state'} before any change "
                      f"({exc.strerror}). {UNCHANGED}") from exc
    executor = executor or SudoExecutor()
    loaded = loaded or _loaded_in_system_domain
    labels = declared_labels() if labels is None else list(labels)
    try:
        for label in labels:
            executor.bootout(label)
        running = agents_running(agents_st.st_uid)
        if running:
            log("rollback is refused, and these processes run as the agents account: " + "; ".join(running)
                + f". Stop them, then run again. {BOOTED}")
            return 2
        still = [label for label in labels if loaded(label)]
        if still:
            log("rollback is refused, and these daemons were booted out but are still loaded: "
                + f"{', '.join(still)}. {BOOTED}")
            return 2
    except Refused as exc:
        raise Refused(f"{exc} {BOOTED}") from exc

    def idle_again() -> None:
        running = agents_running(agents_st.st_uid)
        if running:
            raise Refused("rollback stopped before the tree reached the operator's path, because these processes "
                          "run as the agents account: " + "; ".join(running) + ". The handover had given every "
                          "entry the agents account owned to the operator, with no group or other write bit and no "
                          "ACL, and had removed the links the agents account made inside the tree, so those processes "
                          "can no longer open its entries for writing, though one that already holds a write descriptor "
                          "can still write through it. Stop them, then run again.")

    finished = 0
    try:
        for pair, action in reversed(actions):
            if action == "remote":
                try:
                    _restore_remote(pair, pairs, executor, log=log)
                except Refused as exc:
                    raise Refused(f"{exc} This step changes a checkout's remote, and no tree has changed in it.") from exc
            elif action == "mkdir":
                _undo_container(ag, pair["dst"], log=log)
            elif action == "done":
                log(f"  already reversed: {pair['dst']}")
                continue
            elif action == "remove":
                _undo_copy(ag, pair["dst"], log=log)
            else:
                _undo_move(op, ag, pair["src"], pair["dst"], unlink=action == "link-and-move",
                           files=files, log=log, before_stage=hooks.get("before_stage"),
                           agents_idle=idle_again)
            finished += 1
    except Leftover as exc:
        for line in exc.lines:
            log(f"  not the operator's, so the tree was not moved into place: {line}")
        log(exc.state)
        if finished:
            log(EARLIER)
        log("rollback is not finished. The record is kept, so running it again repeats the handover.")
        return 1
    except Refused as exc:
        if finished:
            raise Refused(f"{exc} {EARLIER}") from exc
        raise
    Path(record).unlink()
    log(f"rolled back the migration of {op}")
    return 0


def declared_labels(repo: Path = REPO) -> list[str]:
    """The launchd labels of every shipped job declaration, read from `jobs/`.

    **THE DECLARATION MODULE MUST COME FROM `repo`'s OWN `tools/`.** Root's copy names `repo` as the
    tree it was checked as, so an import that resolves anywhere else is refused rather than run.
    """
    tools = (repo / "tools").resolve()
    sys.path.insert(0, str(tools))
    from jobs import declaration

    origin = Path(declaration.__file__).resolve()
    if tools not in origin.parents:
        raise Refused(f"jobs.declaration was imported from {origin}, not from {tools}. Nothing was changed.")
    return [declaration.load(path).label for path in sorted((repo / "jobs").glob("*.toml"))]


def run_migration(op: str, ag: str, *, root=None, labels=(), dry_run=False, args=None, executor=None,
                  files_for=None, refusals=None, plan=None, record: Path = RECORD, owners=None,
                  agents=None, log=print) -> int:
    """Migrate the operator's Builder tree into `agents`' home. 0 done, 2 refused.

    `agents` is the (uid, gid) of the `agents` account, and `owners` maps `operator` and
    `agents` to the (uid, gid) each one's files are handed to. Both come from the directory
    service when the caller gives neither, which is how the CLI runs.
    """
    # THE RECORD IS CHECKED BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE, including the operator's root, which is read below.
    refused = record_refusal(record)
    if refused is not None:
        log("the migration is refused, and nothing was changed:")
        log(f"  - {refused}")
        return 2
    op, ag = os.path.abspath(op), os.path.abspath(ag)
    if op == ag:
        raise Refused("the operator's home and `agents`' home are the same directory. "
                      "Nothing was changed.")
    root = root or operator_root(op)
    ident = operator_identity(op)
    reasons = (refusals or migration_refusals)(op, ag, root=root, declared=labels)
    ops = (plan or plan_migration)(op, ag, root=root, labels=labels)
    reasons = reasons + _preflight(ops, op, ag)
    if reasons:
        log("the migration is refused, and nothing was changed:")
        for reason in reasons:
            log(f"  - {reason}")
        return 2
    if dry_run:
        log(f"the migration would run {len(ops)} step(s), and changes nothing:")
        for entry in ops:
            log(f"  {entry['op']}: {_describe(entry)}")
        return 0
    owners = owners or {"operator": (ident["uid"], ident["gid"]),
                        "agents": agents or agents_ids()}
    files = (files_for or RootFiles)(owners)
    executor = executor or SudoExecutor(args)
    data = {"operator_account": ident["account"], "operator_group": ident["group"],
                "operator_home": op, "agents_home": ag, "pairs": []}
    record.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    store_record(record, data)
    moved: dict = {}
    for entry in ops:
        pair = pair_of(entry)
        if pair is not None:
            data["pairs"].append(pair)
            store_record(record, data)
        log(f"  {entry['op']}: {_describe(entry)}")
        try:
            skipped = apply_entry(entry, op=op, ag=ag, files=files, executor=executor, moved=moved)
        except FileNotFoundError as exc:
            raise Refused(f"{exc.filename} went missing during the migration. The record keeps "
                          f"what did complete.") from exc
        for line in skipped:
            log(f"  left as it is: {line}")
    log(f"migrated the Builder tree to {ag}. Verify it with the checks in the runbook's "
        f"step 8 and 9; they are not run here.")
    return 0


def _describe(entry: dict) -> str:
    kind = entry["op"]
    if kind in ("move", "copy", "write"):
        return f"{entry['src']} -> {entry['dst']}" if kind != "write" else entry["dst"]
    if kind == "symlink":
        return f"{entry['link']} -> {entry['target']}"
    if kind in ("mkdir", "rewrite"):
        return entry.get("path", "")
    if kind == "own":
        return f"{entry['path']} to {AGENTS}"
    if kind == "remote":
        return f"{entry['repo']} origin to {entry['after']}"
    if kind == "daemons":
        return ", ".join(entry["labels"]) or "no declared jobs"
    return ""


def root_invocation() -> str:
    """The command a real migration is run with: the installed copy, by absolute path, under the root interpreter.

    The copy is named from the record install-privileged writes, so the message shows the tree root
    will run and not the checkout the operator happened to type from.
    """
    sha = installed_sha(_read_record(INSTALLED_RECORD), "shipwright") or "<sha>"
    interpreter = ROOT_INTERPRETERS["darwin" if sys.platform == "darwin" else "linux"]
    return f"sudo {interpreter} -I {PRIVILEGED_PREFIX}/src/shipwright@{sha}/bin/stand-up-backend"


def run_migration_mode(args, chosen: str | None) -> int:
    """`--migrate-from` and `--rollback-to`. 0 done, 1 failed, 2 refused.

    **A REAL RUN IS ROOT'S.** Its file changes are made in Python as root, so the operator
    runs it under sudo. A dry run changes nothing, so it runs as the operator.
    """
    try:
        if chosen is None and os.geteuid() != 0:
            raise Refused(f"the migration changes files that only root can change. Run it as root from "
                          f"the installed copy, `{root_invocation()} --shape two --migrate-from …`, or "
                          f"`--dry-run` first. Nothing was changed.")
        if args.rollback_to is not None:
            if chosen is not None:
                print(f"{PROG}: --rollback-to takes no report mode; it reverses the "
                      f"recorded migration or refuses. Nothing was changed.", file=sys.stderr)
                return 2
            return rollback(str(args.rollback_to), agents_home(), executor=SudoExecutor(args),
                            labels=declared_labels())
        # THE RECORD IS CHECKED BEFORE THE LABELS OR THE AGENTS HOME ARE READ, as the first step of a migration.
        refused = record_refusal()
        if refused is not None:
            raise Refused(refused)
        return run_migration(str(args.migrate_from), agents_home(), labels=declared_labels(),
                             dry_run=chosen is not None, args=args)
    except Refused as exc:
        print(f"{PROG}: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2


def invoked_path(value: Path | None, start: str) -> Path | None:
    """`value` as a path, a relative one taken from `start`, the directory the command was typed in.

    Root changes to `/` before it parses its arguments, so a relative path has to be resolved
    against `start`, which `main` captured before that change. An absolute path is returned as it is.
    """
    if value is None:
        return None
    return Path(start, value)


def main(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    # THE INVOKING DIRECTORY IS KEPT BEFORE ROOT LEAVES IT, so a relative path argument still names
    # what the operator typed, and not `/`.
    start = os.getcwd()
    # ROOT STARTS IN `/`, BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE. The cwd is the operator's, and an agent may be able to write
    # it: a relative path, or the interpreter's own sys.path[0], would resolve there as root.
    if os.geteuid() == 0:
        os.chdir("/")
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog=PROG,
        description="Stand Builder's backend up by running the four tools in order.",
    )
    parser.add_argument("--shape", choices=tuple(SHAPES),
                        help="the install shape, which this never chooses for you: "
                             + "; ".join(f"{k}, {v}" for k, v in SHAPES.items()))
    parser.add_argument("--deploy", type=Path, default=None,
                        help="deploy root, passed to the steps that place and "
                             "read it (default: each tool's own)")
    parser.add_argument("--jobs", type=Path, default=None,
                        help="directory of job declarations (default: <repo>/jobs)")
    parser.add_argument("--seed-registry", action="store_true",
                        help="give the backend the projects registry the published "
                             "release would, not the one a source checkout carries; "
                             "for anyone installing from source but Convivy itself")
    parser.add_argument("--activate", action="store_true",
                        help="also start the jobs marked enabled-by-default")
    parser.add_argument("--migrate-from", type=Path, default=None, metavar="OPERATOR_HOME",
                        help="move the operator's Builder tree into the agents account (--shape two "
                             "only). A real run is root's: run it from the installed copy, by absolute "
                             "path, as the module usage shows; --dry-run plans it as the operator")
    parser.add_argument("--rollback-to", type=Path, default=None, metavar="OPERATOR_HOME",
                        help="reverse the migration recorded under the root-owned prefix, as root from "
                             "the installed copy (--shape two only)")
    parser.add_argument("--operator-name", default=None,
                        help="the operator's display name, for making them the "
                             "Library's first person (default: git config "
                             "--global user.name, then the account's full "
                             "name, then its login name)")
    mode = parser.add_mutually_exclusive_group()
    mode.add_argument("--status", action="store_true",
                      help="what each step reports, changing nothing")
    mode.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true",
                      help="say what would happen, change nothing")
    mode.add_argument("--check", action="store_true",
                      help="report only; 1 if work is left, 2 if a step refused")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    if os.geteuid() == 0:
        # A PATH ARGUMENT UNDER ROOT NAMES WHAT THE OPERATOR TYPED, NOT `/`.
        args.deploy = invoked_path(args.deploy, start)
        args.jobs = invoked_path(args.jobs, start)
        args.migrate_from = invoked_path(args.migrate_from, start)
        args.rollback_to = invoked_path(args.rollback_to, start)

    if os.geteuid() == 0:
        # ROOT RUNS ONLY ITS OWN COPY, and checks that before it reads anything else: the record,
        # the operator's files and the declarations all come after this.
        problem = root_copy_problem(os.path.abspath(__file__))
        if problem:
            print(f"{PROG}: refusing to run as root from {__file__}: {problem}. Run the copy "
                  f"install-privileged extracted, by its absolute path: {root_invocation()}. "
                  f"Nothing was changed.", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2

    chosen = "status" if args.status else "dry-run" if args.dry_run else \
             "check" if args.check else None

    if args.shape is None:
        shapes = "\n".join(f"  --shape {k:<7} {v}" for k, v in SHAPES.items())
        print(f"{PROG}: name the install shape; this never chooses one for you:\n"
              f"{shapes}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if args.shape == "single" and os.geteuid() == 0:
        # EVERY STEP OF THIS SHAPE WOULD RUN AS ROOT FROM THE OPERATOR'S CHECKOUTS AND COMMANDS, WHICH THE
        # AGENTS ACCOUNT CAN WRITE ONCE A MIGRATION HAS MOVED THEM. THE SINGLE SHAPE RUNS WITHOUT SUDO.
        print(f"{PROG}: --shape single runs every step as the operator. As root, its steps would run "
              f"from the operator's checkouts, which the agents account can write after a migration. "
              f"Run it without sudo. Nothing was changed.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if args.shape == "two":
        # BEFORE ANY STEP, IN EVERY MODE. A reporting mode would otherwise
        # describe installing pieces the two-account shape cannot use.
        lacks = two_shape_lacks()
        if lacks:
            print(f"{PROG}: {refuse_two_shape(lacks)}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2

    if args.migrate_from is not None or args.rollback_to is not None:
        # A MIGRATION IS ITS OWN RUN. It shares the refusal above, not the steps:
        # the steps place and supervise a backend, and a migration moves one that
        # already exists.
        if args.migrate_from is not None and args.rollback_to is not None:
            print(f"{PROG}: name one of --migrate-from and --rollback-to, not both",
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        if args.shape != "two":
            print(f"{PROG}: the migration moves the backend into the `{AGENTS}` account, "
                  f"which only --shape two has. Nothing was changed.", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        return run_migration_mode(args, chosen)

    results: list[dict] = []
    stopped = None
    best = None
    linger_note = None
    for step in steps_for(args.shape):
        # RE-PROBED UNTIL ONE IS FOUND, because `provision` is what installs it.
        # Resolved once before the loop, `best` was None on the very machine
        # this command is written for — a fresh 22.04 box whose `python3` is
        # 3.10 — so `supervise` refused and `place` built its venvs with the
        # stock interpreter, both AFTER provision had just installed 3.11.
        if best is None:
            try:
                best = best_interpreter()
            except Refused as exc:
                # ONLY ROOT RAISES FROM HERE: it names the interpreter it needed, and runs nothing.
                print(f"{PROG}: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
                return 2
        if step["name"] in ("supervise", "library-person") and supervision_skipped(args):
            continue
        if lingers_before(step, chosen, args.shape):
            # The name the process runs as, from its UID. `getpass.getuser()`
            # reads USER and LOGNAME first, which a `su` or `sudo -E` leaves
            # naming someone else.
            linger_note = enable_linger(pwd.getpwuid(os.getuid()).pw_name)
            if linger_note:
                print(f"{PROG}: {linger_note}\n")
        result = run_step(step, args, chosen, best)
        results.append(result)
        # ANY non-zero stops a real run, not only a refusal. A crash exits 1
        # and a signal death is negative, and continuing past either installs
        # onto a machine that cannot run what comes next.
        if result["code"] != 0 and chosen is None:
            stopped = result
            break

    report(results, args, stopped, best, chosen)
    print()
    supervise_ok = any(
        r["step"]["name"] == "supervise" and r["code"] == 0 for r in results
    )
    # Printed again here as well as when it happened, since the steps' output
    # in between scrolls it away and this closing section is what is read.
    if linger_note:
        print(linger_note)
        print()
    for line in outstanding(args, blocks_from(results), chosen, supervise_ok):
        print(line)
    # LAST, since it is the step after this command: pairing Deck with this
    # machine. Read-only, so every mode prints it once the steps have run.
    if stopped is None:
        print()
        for line in closing_pairing(os.geteuid(), pwd.getpwuid(os.getuid()).pw_name, args.shape):
            print(line)

    if stopped is not None:
        print(f"\n{PROG}: the `{stopped['step']['name']}` step said:",
              file=sys.stderr)
        print(stopped["stderr"].strip() or stopped["stdout"].strip(),
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2 if stopped["code"] == 2 else 1
    # ONE MEANING PER CODE, IN EVERY MODE: 2 refused, 1 work left, 0 done.
    # Collapsed to `1 if anything is non-zero`, a refusal and a report of
    # pending work were indistinguishable to a caller.
    if any(r["code"] == 2 for r in results):
        return 2
    if any(r["code"] != 0 for r in results) or supervision_skipped(args):
        return 1
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
